export const BRAIN_NOTES: { path: string; body: string }[] = [{"path":"brain/build-reports/v2-milestone-a.md","body":"---\ntype: build-report\nmilestone: v2-A\ndate: 2026-11-16\nstatus: STOPPED — waiting for Archie\n---\n\n# v2 Milestone A: stop-point report\n\nMilestone A (v2 Phases 1–4) is built and every gate passes on synthetic data in dry-run. Stripe ran in\ntest mode against the fake. **I've stopped here, as planned.** Milestone B (Phases 5–9) stays frozen until\nreal outcomes exist.\n\n## 1. Gate results\n\n| Phase | What it is | Gate | Report |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Outcome ledger, cohorts + blind random holdout, EV send gate, demo-page visits, unit economics with your minutes | **9/9 passed (synthetic)** | `brain/build-reports/v2-phase-1.md`, `docs/gates/v2-phase1.md` |\n| 2 | Workers runtime: Cron → Queue + dead-letter queue, Workflows, recorded external effects, change feed + polling, trigger rules, health page | **8/8 passed** (7 synthetic + Worker build) | `v2-phase-2.md`, `docs/gates/v2-phase2.md` |\n| 3 | Hard budgets in a Durable Object, spend tiers S0–S3, L3 behind your approval, weekly explore share | **5/5 passed (synthetic)** + Worker build | `v2-phase-3.md`, `docs/gates/v2-phase3.md` |\n| 4 | Claim this site (setup + monthly), fulfilment workflow, webhook replay/cancel safety, post-reply (L0) | **7/7 passed (synthetic, test mode)** | `v2-phase-4.md`, `docs/gates/v2-phase4.md` |\n\n**Tests:**\n- `pnpm check` is green: 374 tests in 46 files, typecheck (including the Worker's own) and lint.\n- Each gate also runs in CI at a small size.\n- The Worker builds with every binding (`wrangler deploy --dry-run`): D1, the `JOBS` queue, the\n  `CHANGE_FEED` and `BUDGET` Durable Objects, and the `ENGINE` workflow.\n\n**v2 rules, each enforced in code with a test:**\n\n| Rule | Where it's enforced | Test |\n|---|---|---|\n| 9 Dry-run stays on; Stripe test mode only | kill switch default ON; `assertStripeTestMode`; the real subscription call refuses to run | gate 4 |\n| 10 Synthetic data never trains anything | `synthetic` on tasks, outbox, letters, ledger, claims; the learning filter | unit test + gate 1 |\n| 11 Point-in-time learning | ledger learning filter (`created_at` and `observed_at` ≤ as-of) | unit test |\n| 12 Scoring learns from the ledger only | P(paid) reads the ledger only | behavioural test + static check |\n| 13 The proposer never grades itself | Milestone B (frozen) | — |\n| 14 Agents can't change the safety layer | static checks; owner approvals created only by the dashboard API | `tests/architecture.test.ts` |\n| 15 Truthful labelling | copy rules + writer prompts; reply rules | unit tests |\n| 16 Compliance gates on every send, including holdout | `assessCandidates` shared by ranked and holdout | gate 1 |\n| 17 No repeated external actions | `external_effects`, recorded before every external tool call | gate 2 + unit test |\n\n**Bugs the gates caught and I fixed:**\n- Trigger events past 500 in a batch were silently dropped.\n- A tool refusing *before* acting was logged as \"outcome unknown\", which blocked retries.\n- A company that opted out of letters could still be drawn into the holdout for email. Opt-outs now\n  cover every channel.\n- Two of my own gate checks were too weak, and I tightened them:\n  - the explore-share check passed only because a different cap bound first;\n  - the runner-budget check counted other reservations.\n\n## 2. What I couldn't do from here (needs you, or a session with access)\n\nTwo sets of API docs were **blocked by this build environment's network policy** (the proxy refused\nthem). The rule is to check current docs, not write from memory, so:\n\n1. **Stripe subscriptions.** The claim flow, webhooks and fulfilment are built and tested against the\n   fake Stripe. The two Stripe-specific pieces are isolated and marked:\n   - the real subscription Checkout call **refuses to run**;\n   - the event field names (`toSubscriptionEvent`) are marked **UNVERIFIED**.\n\n   **To finish, either:**\n   - allow `docs.stripe.com` for a session; or\n   - paste the docs for Checkout Sessions (subscription mode), Invoice (`invoice.paid`), Subscription\n     (`customer.subscription.deleted`) and Charge (`charge.refunded`).\n2. **Companies House streaming.** The change feed uses the stream client built in an earlier phase, which\n   was already flagged \"verify at cutover\". I couldn't re-check it. It runs only when you add the\n   `CH_STREAM_KEY` secret; until then polling does the work.\n\n   **To verify it, either:**\n   - allow `developer-specs.company-information.service.gov.uk`; or\n   - check it at cutover (Stage 4).\n\n## 3. Every config value that needs a real number\n\nAll labelled as placeholders or assumptions in the files themselves.\n\n**`packages/config/src/infra.json`**\n\n| Value | Now | What sets it |\n|---|---|---|\n| `measurement.priors.reply_rate` | 0.03 | your first 100–200 real sends (it then learns from the ledger) |\n| `measurement.priors.close_rate` | 0.15 | your history of replies → paid work |\n| `measurement.priors.first_year_value_gbp` | 1500 | claim price: setup + 12 × monthly (£329 with the placeholders below, so set this to match) |\n| `measurement.priors.strength` | 20 | how many sends the prior is \"worth\" |\n| `measurement.holdout_share` | 0.15 | 10–20% allowed; higher answers sooner but costs more random sends |\n| `measurement.letter_ev_k` | 3 | how much better than break-even a letter must look |\n| `measurement.email_send_cost_gbp` | 0.001 | your email provider's price |\n| `measurement.skip_flagged_registered_offices` | true | skip accountant/agent offices at intake (we hold no trading address) |\n| `measurement.demo_visit_window_minutes` | 120 | how long after a visit the time-on-page beacon counts |\n| `caps.spend_tiers` | S1 £10 · S2 £25 · S3 £60 | daily cap per playbook at each tier (S0 = the existing £5) |\n| `caps.explore_share` | 0.10 | experiments' share of the weekly budget |\n| `caps.step_reservation_gbp` | 0.25 | held back before each model step (real steps cost far less) |\n| `letters.print_cost_gbp`, `postage_gbp` | 0.12, 0.87 | your printer and postage (unchanged from v1) |\n| `sender.legal.*` | placeholders | EJTECH's registered name, number and office (real sends refuse until set) |\n\n**`packages/config/src/products.json` → `claim_site`**\n\n| Value | Now | |\n|---|---|---|\n| `setup_fee_gbp` | 149 | your price |\n| `monthly_gbp` | 15 | your price |\n| `details_wait_days` / `publish_wait_days` | 14 / 30 | how long fulfilment waits for the customer, then for you |\n| `human_minutes_target` | 30 | your minutes per site before it shows red |\n| `base_url` | https://sites.ejtech.example | a subdomain of ejtech.co.uk that never sends email (D6) |\n| `terms` / `terms_version` | draft | legal review (below) |\n\n**`packages/config/src/triggers.json`**\n\n| Value | Now | |\n|---|---|---|\n| `fallback_poll.feed_stale_minutes` | 30 | when polling takes over |\n| `fallback_poll.entities_per_poll` | 50 | per 5-minute run |\n| `rules` | 5 rules | which changes act; only \"a lead filed accounts\" calls the model |\n\n**`apps/api/wrangler.jsonc`**\n\n| Value | Now | |\n|---|---|---|\n| cron | every 5 min | how often the loop runs |\n| queue retries | 3, 60 s apart | before a job is set aside as a dead letter |\n| `MODEL_LIVE` | \"\" | \"1\" plus the `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` secret switches the Worker to Claude |\n\n## 4. Legal questions (for a solicitor or the ICO guidance; I can't answer these)\n\n1. **Lawful basis for B2B outreach.** Is legitimate interests right for emailing role addresses (info@,\n   hello@) at limited companies, and for posting letters to \"The Directors\" at the registered office?\n   The checklist now has a legitimate interests assessment (LIA) item covering the opt-out, the 90-day\n   cooldown and the holdout.\n2. **The random holdout.** Contacting a random 15% of eligible companies to measure whether ranking works.\n   Does the LIA need to cover this explicitly? (It uses the same rules, opt-out and labelling as every\n   other send.)\n3. **Demo pages.** We publish a sample site using a company's public Companies House facts, with a \"not\n   affiliated\" banner, noindex, a 30-day expiry and a privacy line. Any passing-off or trade-mark concern\n   with using their registered name on the sample?\n4. **Visit logging on demo pages.** We record open time, QR versus link, and visible time. No cookies,\n   no IP address, no user agent. Does this need consent under PECR regulation 6 (storage/access on the\n   device)? We store nothing on the device, but the script does read page visibility.\n5. **Claim-this-site terms** (draft in `products.json`):\n   - Is \"business-to-business only\" enforceable for sole traders who reach a sample page? We only write\n     to limited companies, but a forwarded letter could reach someone else.\n   - Do we need the consumer cancellation wording (Consumer Contracts Regulations, 14 days) anyway?\n   - Is the 14-day setup-fee refund \"if work hasn't started\" fair?\n6. **ICO registration tier** and whether the registration number should appear on letters.\n7. **Retention.** How long to keep outreach records, suppression entries (indefinitely?) and demo-visit\n   logs.\n\n## 5. Updated cutover checklist\n\n`docs/live-cutover.md` is updated:\n- **Stage 0 (new): legal groundwork.** LIA, ICO registration, privacy notice, demo-page privacy line, and\n  a solicitor's read of the claim terms.\n- **Stage 1: v2 runtime.** D1 migrations 0010–0013; queues and dead-letter queue; Durable Objects and\n  the Workflow; Runtime health and Budgets checks; a forced dead letter.\n- **Stage 3: `MODEL_LIVE`.** Plus the Anthropic key on the Worker.\n- **Stage 4: change feed.** `CH_STREAM_KEY` as a Worker secret, verifying the stream fields the trigger\n  rules use, and reading `triggers.json`.\n- **Stage 6: sending-domain warm-up.** Plus a note on the holdout.\n- **Stage 6b (new): claim this site.** Stripe test mode; the four webhook events; building and verifying\n  the subscription call; the test-mode live runs (pay, details, approve, replay, cancel, your minutes).\n\n## 6. What happens next\n\nNothing, until you say. Milestone B (golden sets, earned autonomy, opportunity cards, brain PRs, the\nloop) stays frozen until real outcomes exist. When you're ready, the useful order is:\n- cutover Stages 0–5 (which you're running in parallel);\n- then 6 and 6b;\n- then a decision on Milestone B once there are a few weeks of real ledger rows.\n\nEvery commit is on `claude/nifty-curie-91l1nq`.\n"},{"path":"brain/build-reports/v2-phase-1.md","body":"---\ntype: build-report\nphase: v2-1\ndate: 2026-11-16\nstatus: gate passed (synthetic)\n---\n\n# v2 Phase 1 — outcome ledger, holdout, EV gate (gate report)\n\n## Revised effort estimate per phase (you asked for this at the top)\n\nPhase 1 was mostly **extension**: the `outcomes` table, cohorts on top of existing intake, and new\nreports over data we already had. Phases 2 and 3 are mostly **new builds** (Queues, Workflows, Durable\nObjects), where the risk sits in Cloudflare behaviour we can only partly test locally. Measured in\nbuild sessions like this one (one session ≈ one long working block, with its tests and report):\n\n| Phase | What it is | Kind | Estimate | Main risk |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 Ledger, holdout, EV gate | extend `outcomes`, cohorts, priors, demo instrumentation, economics | mostly extension | **1 session (done)** | none left; numbers are placeholders until real outcomes |\n| 2 Workers runtime | Cron, Queues + dead-letter queue, a Workflow engine (Cloudflare adapter + a local runner with persisted steps), recorded external effects, change-feed Durable Object with polling fallback, trigger rules, health page | **new build** | **2–3 sessions** | Workflows and Queues can't be fully exercised in Node tests; I'll test the logic through a local runner with the same step contract and run one `wrangler dev` smoke test |\n| 3 Hard budgets | Budget Durable Object (reserve / settle / release), S-tiers with recorded approvals, weekly explore share | **new build** (smaller) | **1–1.5 sessions** | proving \"50 parallel reservations can't exceed a cap\" needs the DO's single-threaded storage; tested against a faithful in-memory version plus one `wrangler dev` run |\n| 4 Claim this site | Stripe test-mode subscription checkout (setup fee + monthly), webhooks, fulfilment Workflow, `post_reply` playbook at L0, terms wording | extension of payments + new workflow | **2 sessions** | consumer cancellation wording needs your legal answer (in the Milestone A report) |\n| Milestone A report | gate results, config values that need real numbers, legal questions, updated cutover checklist | writing | **0.5 session** | — |\n\n**Total remaining for Milestone A: about 6–7 sessions.** Per-playbook evals are Milestone B (frozen), so\nthey're not in this total.\n\n---\n\n## What was built\n\n**Outcome ledger.** The existing `outcomes` table was extended in place (migration 0010; the backtester\nstill works). Every event carries its task, playbook version, cohort and arm, experiment slot, a £ value\n(actual for money events, otherwise an *estimate with its assumption written next to it*), time on page,\n`synthetic` and `dry_run` flags, and a dedupe key so a retry never double-counts. The events written\ntoday:\n\n| Event | Where it's recorded |\n|---|---|\n| `sent` (email) | the `send_email` tool, once per email (dry runs are flagged `dry_run`) |\n| `sent` (letter) | approving a print batch: one per approved letter |\n| `qr_scan`, `demo_visit` | serving a live demo page (`?s=qr` on the printed QR code tells a scan from a typed link) |\n| `reply`, `reply_positive` | the inbox loop, attributed to the task that sent the original email |\n| `booking`, `paid`, `refunded`, `churned` | entered by hand on `POST /api/ledger` for now; Stripe writes `paid` from Phase 4 |\n\n**Cohorts and the holdout (decision D4).** Each intake of approved outreach leads opens a cohort with a\nrecorded seed. Ranked leads go through the same compliance checks as before, then the EV gate. A random\nholdout of `ranked × h / (1 − h)` companies (h = 15%) is then drawn from the eligible universe, by the\nseed. The holdout gets the same playbook for its channel and **passes the same compliance checks**. The\nEV gate isn't applied to it, otherwise it wouldn't be random. Holdout drafts are **blind** in Review: the\nlist shows only the company number, and a waiting task hides its reference, idempotency key and arm.\nLift is measured on **assignment** (intent to treat), with a 95% interval. Drafts you rejected are\nreported separately.\n\n**P(paid) and the EV send gate.** P(reply) and P(paid | reply) start from priors in config, all labelled\nas assumptions: 3% reply, 15% close, £1,500 first-year value, worth 20 sends. They move towards real\nrates as sends accumulate (Beta-binomial). They read **only** the ledger, and only rows that are real\n(`synthetic = 0`), not dry runs, and recorded before the as-of time.\n- **Letters** go only when EV ≥ 3 × the letter's cost. With the priors, EV is\n  0.03 × 0.15 × £1,500 − £0.99 = £5.76, against a threshold of 3 × £0.99 = £2.97.\n- **Email** goes when EV > 0.\n\n**Demo pages.**\n- The page records that it was opened, whether by QR or link, and how long it was visible.\n- **No cookies, no IP address, no user agent.**\n- One inline script, allowed by its hash in the Content-Security-Policy, which may only talk back to our\n  own server.\n- A privacy line has been added to the page footer.\n\n**Unit economics and your minutes.** Per playbook (and per experiment, from Phase 3) the system shows\nmodel cost, send cost (print and postage, plus real emails), revenue, margin and **your minutes**.\n- The dashboard times each review decision, counting only time the page was visible.\n- A print batch's time is split across its letters.\n- These figures appear on Metrics as \"Ranked leads against a random holdout\" and \"Unit economics\", with\n  an \"Include simulated rows\" switch that labels everything SIMULATED.\n\n**Truthful labelling (rule 15).** Both writer prompts now say the message is a sales approach. The checker\nrejects any draft that poses as a survey, research or a personal note. The phrase list is in config.\n\n**Simulator.** `AgentService` takes `synthetic: true`. Every task, outbox row, letter, ledger row and\nreview session it creates is then marked synthetic. The Phase 1 gate runs through it in a sandbox database.\n\n## Changes in behaviour you should know about\n\n1. **A company that asked for no more letters is now not contacted by email either.** Before, its\n   suppression only stopped letters. \"One global suppression list\" should mean any channel, and the gate\n   caught a holdout email going to such a company. Logged in `docs/decisions.md`.\n2. **Leads at an accountant's or formation agent's registered office are now skipped at intake**, not\n   just flagged in the print batch. We hold no trading address for them, and a letter there rarely\n   reaches the directors. This is a config switch (`measurement.skip_flagged_registered_offices`), and the\n   skip is listed in the cohort with its reason. The Phase 5 gate was updated to expect this.\n3. **Holdout tasks appear in Review and the print batch alongside ranked ones**, about 15% extra volume.\n   That's the cost of being able to say whether ranking works.\n\n## Gate (docs/gates/v2-phase1.md)\n\nRun with `pnpm gate:v2-phase1`: 1,023 synthetic companies, 20 email leads and 12 letter leads approved.\n\n| Criterion | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Every outreach intake is a cohort: ranked + random holdout at the configured share | passed (synthetic) |\n| The holdout draw is random but reproducible from its recorded seed | passed (synthetic) |\n| Holdout sends pass the same compliance checks | passed (synthetic) |\n| Blind review: holdout drafts are indistinguishable from ranked ones | passed (synthetic) |\n| Outcome ledger: sent, QR scan, demo visit (+ time on page), reply, paid — each attributed | passed (synthetic) |\n| Demo pages: no cookies/IP/device details; one hashed script; privacy line | passed (synthetic) |\n| Synthetic data never trains anything; reports show it only when asked, labelled | passed (synthetic) |\n| Lift on assignment with a 95% interval; no claim without paid outcomes | passed (synthetic) |\n| Unit economics per playbook incl. your minutes | passed (synthetic) |\n\n**Tests:** `pnpm check` is green: typecheck, lint and 356 tests in 41 files. The new guardrail tests are\nin `packages/infra/src/__tests__/v2-measurement.test.ts`:\n- synthetic and dry-run rows never move P(paid);\n- point-in-time (outcomes after as-of are ignored);\n- your approvals, edits and review time never change P(paid), checked by behaviour and by a static check\n  over the scoring and targeting code;\n- the EV gate;\n- seed reproducibility;\n- holdout size;\n- the lift interval;\n- truthful labelling;\n- demo page instrumentation.\n\nThe simulator gate also runs in CI at a small size (`apps/api/src/gates/v2-phase1.test.ts`).\n\n## Placeholders that need real numbers (collected again in the Milestone A report)\n\n| Config (`packages/config/src/infra.json` → `measurement`) | Now | What would set it |\n|---|---|---|\n| `priors.reply_rate` | 0.03 | your first 100–200 real sends |\n| `priors.close_rate` | 0.15 | your own history of replies that turned into paid work |\n| `priors.first_year_value_gbp` | 1500 | the claim-this-site price (setup + 12 × monthly) once you set it in Phase 4 |\n| `priors.strength` | 20 | how much to trust the prior; 20 = \"as much as 20 real sends\" |\n| `holdout_share` | 0.15 | 10–20% allowed; higher gives an answer sooner but costs more sends to random companies |\n| `letter_ev_k` | 3 | how much better than break-even a letter must look |\n| `email_send_cost_gbp` | 0.001 | your sending provider's real per-email price |\n\n## Not done in Phase 1 (by design)\n\n- Stripe writes `paid` automatically from Phase 4 (claim this site). Until then, enter it on\n  `POST /api/ledger`.\n- No per-lead adjustment of P(paid) yet. It's one estimate per playbook until outcomes can support more.\n- Nothing learns yet. The estimates move as real outcomes arrive, but weights don't change; that's\n  Milestone B.\n\nNext: Phase 2 (Workers runtime).\n"},{"path":"brain/build-reports/v2-phase-2.md","body":"---\ntype: build-report\nphase: v2-2\ndate: 2026-11-16\nstatus: gate passed (synthetic + Worker build)\n---\n\n# v2 Phase 2: Workers runtime (gate report)\n\n## In one paragraph\n\nProduction now has everything it needs to run on Cloudflare Workers (decision D3).\n- **Schedule and queue.** A scheduled trigger fires every 5 minutes and puts jobs on a queue. A consumer\n  runs them. A job that keeps failing is retried 3 times, then set aside on a dead-letter queue, which\n  raises an alert.\n- **Workflows.** Multi-step work that spans days runs as Cloudflare Workflows. A live workflow stops\n  within one step when you turn the kill switch on, and carries on from the same place when you turn it\n  off.\n- **No repeated external actions.** Every external action is written down before it happens. A retry\n  after a crash gets the first result back instead of acting twice. If nobody can tell whether an action\n  happened, it's never retried automatically: it waits on the health page for you to say.\n- **Change feed and triggers.** A Durable Object is ready to follow the Companies House change feed, with\n  polling as the fallback. Change events pass through cheap rules first, so the model is only called for\n  the few that matter.\n- **Locally**, the same jobs run in Node against SQLite.\n\n## What was built\n\n| Part | Where | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cron → queue → consumer | `wrangler.jsonc`, `apps/api/src/runtime-worker.ts` | every 5 min: agent loop, polling fallback, workflow wake-ups |\n| Dead-letter queue | `ae-jobs-dlq` | 3 retries (60 s apart), then recorded in `dead_letters` + alert |\n| Job handler | `apps/agent-runner/src/jobs.ts` | one `handleJob` for Worker and Node; `LocalJobQueue` copies the retry/DLQ rules |\n| Workflow engine | `packages/infra/src/workflow.ts` | one step contract; Cloudflare adapter `EngineWorkflow`; local runner persists steps in `workflow_steps` |\n| No repeated external actions (rule 17) | `packages/infra/src/tools.ts`, table `external_effects` | recorded before the call; done → result replayed; unknown → refused until a person settles it |\n| Change feed | `ChangeFeed` Durable Object + `change-feed.ts` | holds the feed position; delivers before moving it; reads the existing stream client when `CH_STREAM_KEY` is set (to verify at cutover) |\n| Polling fallback | `pollFallback` | takes over when the feed is missing or quiet for 30 min; refreshes leads and watched companies in rotation and turns changes into the same events |\n| Trigger rules | `packages/config/src/triggers.json`, `triggers.ts` | 5 rules; only `explain` calls the model; counts per rule per day in `trigger_stats` |\n| Health page | `/api/runtime`, Controls → Runtime health | heartbeats, feed state, failed jobs, uncertain actions (with \"It happened\" / \"It didn't happen\"), paused or errored workflows, trigger counts |\n| Real model switch | Worker var `MODEL_LIVE` | the Worker uses the fake model unless `MODEL_LIVE=1` **and** the `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` secret are set. Models are unchanged. |\n\n**Workflows registered so far: none.** The engine is ready. Phase 4 adds claim-this-site fulfilment, and\nthe weekly and monthly loops are Milestone B. The gate exercises the engine with a fixture workflow.\n\n## Gate (docs/gates/v2-phase2.md): `pnpm gate:v2-phase2`\n\n| Criterion | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Global kill switch stops a live workflow within one step; resume continues where it stopped | passed (synthetic) |\n| Kill/resume and a crash after an external action cause no repeated action | passed (synthetic) |\n| An action whose outcome is unknown is never repeated automatically; a person settles it | passed (synthetic) |\n| 1,000 change events: the model is called only for rule matches (110 matched → 3 model calls, all leads) | passed (synthetic) |\n| Queue: a failing job is retried, then dead-lettered with an alert; other jobs run | passed (synthetic) |\n| Change feed not connected → polling fallback feeds the same trigger rules | passed (synthetic) |\n| Health page shows heartbeats, dead letters, uncertain actions, workflows, trigger counts | passed (synthetic) |\n| The Worker builds with its Cron, Queue + DLQ, Durable Object and Workflow (`wrangler deploy --dry-run`) | passed |\n\n**Tests:** `pnpm check` is green: 364 tests in 43 files, and the Worker's own typecheck. New tests:\n`packages/infra/src/__tests__/v2-runtime.test.ts` and `apps/api/src/gates/v2-phase2.test.ts`.\n\n**A bug the gate caught:** the trigger processor silently ignored events past 500 in one batch. It now\nprocesses everything it's given, and the batch size is applied when events are put on the queue (split\ninto several messages).\n\n## What's NOT done, and why\n\n1. **The Companies House stream isn't verified yet.** *(Corrected after Phase 4.)* An earlier phase had\n   already built a stream client, `HttpChangeStream`, with its host, paths, auth and event shape flagged\n   \"to verify at cutover\". The change feed now uses it, through `streamFeedSource`, whenever the\n   `CH_STREAM_KEY` Worker secret is set. Without the key, the health page says \"change feed: not\n   connected\" and polling does the work.\n\n   I couldn't re-check the streaming docs here: the docs host and the stream host are both blocked by\n   this build environment's network policy. Verifying them stays on the cutover checklist. The stream\n   needs its own **stream key** from Companies House, which is different from the REST API key.\n2. **Cloudflare delivery itself hasn't run for real.** Queue retries, Workflow scheduling and Durable\n   Object alarms are configuration plus thin adapters. They're checked by the Worker build, and get their\n   first real run when you deploy (in the cutover checklist). The logic they call is tested locally.\n3. **Polling covers leads and watched companies, not the whole universe**: 50 per 5-minute run, in\n   rotation, about 14,000 a day. That's within the Companies House API's limit. The change feed is what\n   covers everything.\n\n## New config values that need your eye\n\n| Config | Now | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `triggers.json` → `fallback_poll.feed_stale_minutes` | 30 | how long without feed events before polling takes over |\n| `triggers.json` → `fallback_poll.entities_per_poll` | 50 | companies refreshed per 5-minute run while polling |\n| `triggers.json` → `rules` | 5 rules | which changes act; only \"a lead filed accounts → re-explain the lead\" calls the model |\n| `wrangler.jsonc` → cron | every 5 min | how often the loop runs |\n\nNext: Phase 3 (hard budgets in a Durable Object, S-tiers, explore share).\n"},{"path":"brain/build-reports/v2-phase-3.md","body":"---\ntype: build-report\nphase: v2-3\ndate: 2026-11-16\nstatus: gate passed (synthetic + Worker build)\n---\n\n# v2 Phase 3: hard budgets (gate report)\n\n## In one paragraph\n\nSpend caps used to be checked against the run log **after** money was spent, so several jobs running at\nonce could each see room and together go over. Now every model step **reserves** money first, in one\nBudget Durable Object, and settles at the real cost afterwards (or releases the reservation if the step\nfails). The Durable Object handles one request at a time, so 50 jobs asking at the same moment can't\noverspend a cap. Each playbook has a **spend tier** (S0 to S3):\n- you can lower a tier with one click;\n- raising one records your approval first, and nothing else can raise it;\n- **L3 autonomy** now needs the same recorded approval.\n\n**Exploratory work** (experiments and explore-flagged leads) is capped at **10% of the week's budget**,\nso it can't crowd out normal work.\n\n## What was built\n\n| Part | Where |\n|---|---|\n| Budget ledger (reserve / settle / release, all-or-nothing across scopes) | `packages/infra/src/budget.ts` → `BudgetBook` |\n| Budget Durable Object (SQLite-backed, synchronous KV) | `apps/api/src/runtime-worker.ts` → `BudgetObject`, binding `BUDGET` |\n| Local equivalent (same ledger behind an in-process lock) | `LocalBudgetPort` |\n| Runner integration: each step holds min(£0.25, what the task has left), then settles | `packages/infra/src/runner.ts` |\n| Scopes: task budget · playbook daily cap (by tier) · global daily cap · weekly explore share | `scopesFor` |\n| Spend tiers S0–S3 + owner approvals (single-use, who/when recorded) | `setSpendTier`, table `owner_approvals` (migration 0012) |\n| L3 needs a recorded approval | `promotePlaybook` |\n| Reporting | table `budget_events`, `/api/budgets`, Controls → Budgets (tier selector) |\n| Rule 14 checks | `tests/architecture.test.ts`: agent-facing code can't touch switches, caps, tiers, autonomy or approvals; approvals are created only by the dashboard API |\n\nWhen a reservation is refused it works like a cap being hit:\n- the queue pauses (unless only the task's own budget is full);\n- you get an alert;\n- the step stops.\n\nA step that costs more than it reserved is recorded at the real cost, and raises a \"budget overrun\"\nalert.\n\n## Gate (docs/gates/v2-phase3.md): `pnpm gate:v2-phase3`\n\n| Criterion | Status | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| 50 parallel reservations can't exceed a cap | passed (synthetic) | £5 cap, 50 × £0.25 at once → exactly 20 granted, 30 refused, queue paused + alert |\n| S-tier increases need your recorded approval (lowering doesn't) | passed (synthetic) | no approval, made-up approval and reused approval all refused; dashboard approval → raised; lowering free |\n| L3 autonomy needs your recorded approval | passed (synthetic) | L2 → L3 refused without it |\n| Experiment spend stays within the weekly explore share | passed (synthetic) | £14.00 share: £14.00 spent, then refused; normal work still got budget |\n| The runner reserves before every step and settles at the real cost | passed (synthetic) | 5 steps → 5 reservations, all settled |\n\nThe Worker also builds with the new `BUDGET` Durable Object (`wrangler deploy --dry-run`).\n\n**Tests:** `pnpm check` is green: 367 tests in 44 files.\n\n**A gate weakness I fixed while writing it:** the first version of the explore check passed only because\nthe playbook's own daily cap ran out first. The explore share was never actually tested. The gate now\nraises that playbook to S3 (through the approval path) so the explore share is the real limit.\n\n## Config values that need real numbers\n\n| Config (`infra.json` → `caps`) | Now | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `global_per_day_gbp` | 20 | everything, per day (unchanged) |\n| `per_playbook_per_day_gbp` | 5 | the S0 cap per playbook (unchanged) |\n| `spend_tiers` | S1 £10 · S2 £25 · S3 £60 | daily cap per playbook at each tier |\n| `explore_share` | 0.10 | experiments' share of the weekly budget (global × 7) |\n| `step_reservation_gbp` | 0.25 | held back before each step (assumption; real steps cost well under 1p with the fake model) |\n\n## Notes\n\n- Printing and postage still don't count against the daily caps (you approve those per batch). They do\n  count in task cost and margins. Unchanged from before.\n- The old after-the-fact cap check is still there as a second line of defence.\n\nNext: Phase 4 (claim-this-site checkout, fulfilment workflow, post-reply playbook).\n"},{"path":"brain/build-reports/v2-phase-4.md","body":"---\ntype: build-report\nphase: v2-4\ndate: 2026-11-16\nstatus: gate passed (synthetic, test mode)\n---\n\n# v2 Phase 4: claim this site, fulfilment, post-reply (gate report)\n\n## In one paragraph\n\nA company that likes its sample page can now click **\"Like it? Claim this site\"** and do three things:\n- read the price and the terms;\n- pay a **setup fee plus a monthly subscription** through Stripe (test mode only);\n- get its site built.\n\nThe build runs as a **fulfilment workflow**:\n1. Email the customer a private details form, then wait up to 14 days.\n2. Draft the site **only from what they sent**.\n3. Wait for **your approval** on the dashboard.\n4. Publish it.\n5. Email them an offer to connect their own domain.\n\nIf they cancel or are refunded at any point, fulfilment stops at its next step and the site comes down.\n\nEvery payment, renewal, cancellation and refund lands in the outcome ledger, **attributed to the letter\nor email that earned it**. That's what lift and margins will be measured on.\n\nSeparately, replies to our outreach now get a **post-reply** draft: triage happens in code, the model\ndrafts an answer, and it waits for your review. Nothing is sent, because it's L0.\n\n## What was built\n\n| Part | Where |\n|---|---|\n| Claim page, terms (draft), form → subscription checkout | `/claim/:slug`, `POST /public/claim/:slug`, `startClaim` |\n| \"Claim this site\" link on every live sample page | `instrumentDemoHtml` |\n| Stripe subscription events → claim, ledger, workflow (idempotent) | `handleClaimEvent`: paid, renewal, cancelled (churned), refunded |\n| Fulfilment workflow `claim_fulfilment` + playbook spec `claim_site` (L0) | `packages/infra/src/claim.ts`, `playbooks/claim_site/SKILL.md` |\n| Workflow \"wait for event\" (details form, your approval, cancellation) | `waitForEvent` in both engines (Cloudflare `step.waitForEvent`, local events table) |\n| Customer tools: `send_customer_email`, `publish_site` (operator only, recorded before they run) | `registerClaimTools` |\n| Details form and the live site | `/claim/details/:token`, `/s/:slug` |\n| Dashboard: Claimed sites (draft, Approve / Not yet, your minutes vs the 30-minute target) | Letters page → Claimed sites |\n| `post_reply` playbook (L0): code triage → draft (their message is untrusted) → checks → your review | `playbooks/post_reply`, `packages/infra/src/playbooks/post_reply.ts` |\n| Test mode only (rule 9) | a live Stripe key switches card payments off |\n\n## Gate (docs/gates/v2-phase4.md): `pnpm gate:v2-phase4`\n\n| Criterion | Status |\n|---|---|\n| End to end in test mode: sample → checkout (setup + monthly) → paid → details → draft → approved → live → domain proposal | passed (synthetic) |\n| Ledger attribution: checkout_started, paid (setup + first month) and the renewal → the letter that earned them | passed (synthetic) |\n| Webhook replay doesn't double-fulfil | passed (synthetic) |\n| Cancellation (opt-out) stops fulfilment within one step; refund recorded | passed (synthetic) |\n| post_reply at L0: triaged, checked draft for review; nothing sent; the reply's instructions are ignored | passed (synthetic) |\n| Stripe test mode only; the unverified real subscription call refuses to run | passed (synthetic) |\n| Your minutes per site against the target | passed (synthetic) |\n\n**Tests:** `pnpm check` is green: 373 tests in 46 files. The Worker builds with all bindings.\n\n**Fixes the gate drove:**\n- A tool that refuses **before** acting (for example, trading details still placeholders) used to be\n  logged as \"outcome unknown\", which then blocked every retry. Tools now signal \"didn't happen\" in that\n  case. Errors after acting still need a person to check.\n\n## What's NOT done, and why\n\n1. **The real Stripe subscription call isn't built, and Stripe's event field names aren't verified.**\n   Stripe's docs were blocked from the build environment, and the rule is not to write API calls from\n   memory. The real `createSubscriptionCheckout` **refuses to run**. Field names in\n   `toSubscriptionEvent` are clearly marked UNVERIFIED. To finish it, either:\n   - allow `docs.stripe.com` for a session; or\n   - paste the Checkout Sessions (subscription mode), Invoice, Subscription and Charge docs.\n2. **The terms are a draft.** The wording in `products.json` → `claim_site.terms` needs your solicitor's\n   eye. The questions are in the Milestone A report.\n3. **Hosting is minimal.** A published site is a simple page at `/s/:slug` built from their details. A\n   proper template, images and the custom-domain set-up are later work (the domain is a proposal email\n   for now).\n\n## Config values that need real numbers (`products.json` → `claim_site`)\n\n| Value | Now | |\n|---|---|---|\n| `setup_fee_gbp` | 149 | placeholder |\n| `monthly_gbp` | 15 | placeholder |\n| `details_wait_days` | 14 | how long to wait for their details form |\n| `publish_wait_days` | 30 | how long a draft waits for your review |\n| `human_minutes_target` | 30 | your minutes per site before it shows red |\n| `base_url` | https://sites.ejtech.example | placeholder (D6): becomes a subdomain of ejtech.co.uk that never sends email |\n| `terms`, `terms_version` | draft-2026-11-16 | needs legal review |\n\nNext: the Milestone A report, then stop.\n"},{"path":"brain/build-reports/v2-plan.md","body":"# Agent Engine v2 — plan (for Archie's approval)\n\nWritten 2026-11-16. **No code has been written for v2.** This is step 0 of the v2 prompt: audit, schema\nchanges, new files, risks and the three questions. It ends with the decisions I need from you.\n\n> **The feature freeze is still in force.** On 2026-11-10 you froze feature work until cutover Stage 5\n> (real drafts) is done, and confirmed it again since. v2 is feature work. Approving this plan needs to\n> say explicitly that v2 overrides the freeze (and whether the dry-run v2 build should happen before or\n> after Stage 5). Until then I'll only answer questions about this plan.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the v2 prompt assumes vs what is actually in the repo\n\nSome things the prompt says \"already exist\" don't, or exist in a different shape. Existing code wins on\nnames; the gaps below change the size of the build.\n\n| The prompt assumes | Reality |\n|---|---|\n| Cloudflare Queues with a dead-letter queue | **Missing.** Only a `QueueBus` port with `InMemoryQueue`. `wrangler.jsonc` has no queues, Durable Objects, Workflows or R2. Failed tasks end as `status = failed` (shown on Controls), with no DLQ. |\n| Per-playbook evals | **Missing.** There's the 150-company *verification* golden set (`golden` table, Golden page) and synthetic phase gates. No playbook eval harness. |\n| A status-and-vision doc and \"revenue paths 3–6\" | **Not in the repo.** `CLAUDE.md` lists 4 revenue items: 1 SME lead gen, 2 x402, 3 monitoring + human check, 4 later (Agent House, M&A). **Path 3 is already built.** I need your list (decision D2). |\n| `brain/` holds build reports | Build reports, decisions and the cutover checklist live in `docs/` (`docs/gates/`, `docs/decisions.md`, `docs/live-cutover.md`). I'll write v2 reports to `brain/build-reports/` as asked and keep `docs/` as is, linking both ways. |\n| A sixteen-function business taxonomy in `brain/` | **Not there.** `brain/` has two framework notes (`lead_gen_sme_web`, `x402_discovery`) and empty `digests/` and `signals/`. You'll need to supply the list (Phase 7 only, so not blocking Milestone A). |\n| Tool registry, spend caps, kill switches, licence lanes, operator never sees raw content, cost attribution, idempotent `createTask`, review queue, L0–L3 with auto-demotion | **Present** (`packages/infra`: `tools.ts`, `controls.ts`, `runner.ts`, `autonomy.ts`, `tasks.ts`, `contact.ts`; `packages/verification/src/query.ts` for lanes). Note: promotion is *suggested* and needs your click today; v2 Phase 6 makes L0→L2 automatic. |\n\n## 2. Audit per phase\n\nLegend: **exists** / **partial** / **missing**.\n\n### Milestone A\n\n**Phase 1 — Outcome ledger**\n\n| Item | Status | Where / what's missing |\n|---|---|---|\n| `outcomes` table | partial | `outcomes` (migration 0004): `opportunity_id, entity_id, module, event, value_bool, value_gbp, observed_at, source ∈ {human, system, verified_fact}`. Used by the backtester and calibration. Missing: `task_id`, `playbook`, `playbook_version`, `experiment_id`, `hypothesis_id`, funnel event types, estimate-vs-actual + assumption, `synthetic`, new sources (stripe, demo, inbox, simulator). **Extend this table** (rebuild migration, because SQLite can't change a CHECK constraint); keep the backtester working. |\n| Other money/activity records | exists | `outbox` (emails), `payments_ledger` (x402, Stripe), `x402_requests`, `watch_alerts`, `letters`, `run_log` (cost per step), `tasks.cost_gbp/revenue_gbp`, `margins` view (Metrics). The ledger becomes the one funnel view; these stay as the detailed records. |\n| Demo-page instrumentation | partial | Each letter already has an unguessable demo slug and opt-out code. Missing: visit logging, time on page, QR-vs-typed source. No cookies are used today. |\n| Holdout control | missing | Module config has `explore_share` (exploration among *ranked* leads), which is not a random unranked holdout. |\n| EV send gate | partial | `opportunities.expected_value_gbp` = base_win_rate × score/0.5 × deal value (from the framework note's `economics`). Missing: send cost, P(paid), letter `k` gate, the \"skip registered/accountant offices\" rule (today these are only *flagged*). |\n| Cold-start priors | partial | `economics: { deal_value_gbp: 1500, base_win_rate: 0.02 }` in the framework note. Missing: separate reply/close priors, labelling as assumptions, Bayesian update. |\n| Unit economics + human minutes | partial | Cost, revenue and margin per playbook exist. **Human minutes: missing.** |\n| Synthetic flag | missing | Synthetic data lives in separate databases today (gates, screenshots); no row-level `synthetic` flag. v2 needs it on every row that learning reads. |\n\n**Phase 2 — Runtime**\n\n| Item | Status | Where / what's missing |\n|---|---|---|\n| Workflows | missing | — |\n| Queues + DLQ | missing | Port exists (`QueueBus`); real binding and DLQ don't. |\n| Never repeat an external action | exists | Tool idempotency (outbox, ledger references, Stripe event idempotency, x402 settle-after-success). Workflows steps will reuse it. |\n| Reactive triggers | partial | Companies House streaming consumer (`verification/.../stream.ts`) turns events into re-verification requests. Missing: queue, coded trigger rules, the \"model only when a rule fires\" accounting. |\n| Health page | partial | Controls shows caps, kill switches, alerts, outbox. Missing: running workflows, stuck steps, DLQ. |\n\n**Phase 3 — Hard budgets**\n\n| Item | Status | Where / what's missing |\n|---|---|---|\n| Spend caps | partial | `checkSpend` sums `run_log` in D1 per playbook/day and globally; on breach it pauses the queue and alerts. **Not strongly consistent** (two parallel tasks can both pass). Missing: reserve/settle, Durable Objects. |\n| S-tiers, explore share (portfolio) | missing | — |\n\n**Phase 4 — Taking money**\n\n| Item | Status | Where / what's missing |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stripe Checkout + signed, idempotent webhook | exists | `ports/payments.ts` (`StripeCheckout`, `verifyStripeWebhook`), `products/stripe-events.ts`, human report (£9) and monitoring. Missing: subscriptions (monitoring v1 is one-off; noted as a cutover item), setup fee + monthly hosting, refunds/churn events. |\n| \"Claim this site\" | missing | Demo pages exist (`/demo/:slug`, banner, noindex, 30-day expiry, disclosures). |\n| Fulfilment | missing | No site hosting, content editing or publishing. Closest pattern: `human_report` (paid → L3 playbook → delivered). |\n| Post-reply playbook | partial | `processInbox` stops follow-ups, handles opt-outs (email threads and letter codes) and records replies as outcomes. Missing: triage into 5 classes, drafted answers checked against facts, claim/booking links. No real inbox adapter yet (cutover item). |\n| Compliance on demo pages | exists | Banner, disclosures, noindex, opt-out, expiry. Missing: checkout terms, consumer cancellation wording, privacy notice for visit logging. |\n\n### Milestone B (brief; detailed plan after Milestone A)\n\n| Phase | Status | Closest existing parts |\n|---|---|---|\n| 5 Golden set (playbooks) | partial | `approvals` + `examples` tables already store every approve/edit/reject with the edited text; the verification golden set has a harness pattern. Missing: promotion to golden cases, hidden holdout, `pnpm eval`, CI release gate. |\n| 6 Earned autonomy | partial | `autonomy.ts` (`playbookStats`, promotion suggestion, demotion on breach), Controls page. Missing: automatic L0→L2, ledger-based margin windows, `MIN_EXPECTED_PAID`, fix-or-kill. |\n| 7 Opportunity cards | partial | The opportunity engine (`runModule`, framework notes, traced scores, explanations) is the scoring half. Missing: fields config, taxonomy, scouts, hypothesis backlog, cards, experiments, injection suite (website extraction already treats pages as untrusted and is tested against injected instructions; reuse). |\n| 8 Brain PRs | partial | `pnpm weights suggest` writes a suggested change into a *copy* of the framework note (never applied silently). Missing: bot PRs, path allowlist CI, branch protection. |\n| 9 The loop | partial | Weekly digests and margin report (`pnpm digests`), nightly job. Missing: task write-back table, monthly portfolio card, model-check command. |\n\n## 3. The three questions\n\n### Q1. Where does `apps/agent-runner` run, and which jobs move to Workflows?\n\n**Today:** a Node service, run locally against SQLite with fakes. `CLAUDE.md` and the Phase 2 plan say\n\"Cloudflare Containers at cutover\". It has never been deployed.\n\n**Recommendation: don't deploy the Node runner. Move all its production jobs onto Workers.** Everything\nit does is plain TypeScript that already runs in a Worker bundle: the playbook runner, the tool\nregistry, the Anthropic client (`@anthropic-ai/sdk` uses `fetch`), PDF and QR rendering (pdf-lib,\nqrcode, already in the Worker bundle), and the D1 storage adapter. A Container would add a second\nruntime, a database bridge back to D1, and its own cost, for no capability we need.\n\n| Runner job today | v2 home |\n|---|---|\n| `tick()` loop: intake approved leads, apply decisions, run queued tasks, follow-ups, inbox poll, watch alerts, weekly print batch | **Cron Trigger** every few minutes that enqueues work on a **Queue**; queue consumer runs short tasks |\n| Long-running, multi-step work: fulfilment (P4), experiments (P7), weekly/monthly loops (P9) | **Workflows** (one class each) |\n| Companies House streaming consumer (long-lived HTTP stream) | Workers can't hold an open stream indefinitely. Options: (a) a small **Durable Object** that holds the stream and re-connects from its saved timepoint; (b) keep it in Node on a container. **I recommend (a)** and will verify DO limits on long outbound streams when building Phase 2; if they don't fit, fall back to polling the CH API by timepoint from a Cron Trigger. |\n| Bulk ingestion (500 MB CH bulk file, accounts iXBRL zips) and the rehearsal | **Stays Node, run by hand** (your laptop or a one-off container) — it's a monthly batch job, not a service. |\n| Local development and tests | Node + SQLite + fakes, as today; Workflow and DO classes are thin wrappers around pure functions that are tested in Node, plus Miniflare bundle tests like the existing Worker test. |\n\nThis changes a line in `CLAUDE.md` (\"Claude Agent SDK runs in a Node service\") — we never used the\nAgent SDK (decision logged in Phase 2); I'd update `CLAUDE.md` to say production runs on Workers,\nQueues, Workflows and Durable Objects. **Decision D3.**\n\n### Q2. How big does D1 get?\n\nMeasured on the Phase 1 rehearsal database (synthetic, same schema): **18,061 companies in the anchor\nregistry → 552 MB after one ingest**, i.e. **~30 KB per company**. Where it goes: claims 149 MB, fact\nversions 104 MB, current facts 70 MB, observations 38 MB, plus indexes (~45% of the total).\n\nGrowth comes from history, not from new companies. A re-fetch adds an observation (~0.3 KB with its\nindexes) per claim; a changed value adds a claim and a fact version (~1 KB). Website facts add roughly\nhalf again on top of the Companies House data.\n\n| Scope | Companies (anchor registry) | Day one | Per year of history (quarterly re-checks + changes) | Outcome ledger + demo visits |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Greater Manchester (today) | ~18k | **~0.8 GB** (incl. websites) | **~0.6 GB/yr** | ~0.05 GB/yr at 500 sends/week |\n| UK, same filters (5–100 staff) | ~0.9–1M (estimate: ~50× GM) | **~40 GB** | **~25–30 GB/yr** | ~1–2 GB/yr |\n\nD1's limit is **10 GB per database, hard** (1 TB per account), and from 4 Nov 2026 a single query may\nreturn at most 50 MB.\n\n- **Greater Manchester fits in one database for ~10 years.** Nothing to build now. I'll add a size\n  check to the health page that warns at 6 GB.\n- **UK scale does not fit and needs a split** (not in this build; UK is out of scope):\n  1. **Hot database** (one D1): current facts, entities, tasks, outcomes, letters, controls — everything\n     the dashboard and runner touch. Current facts are ~5 KB/company → ~5 GB at UK scale.\n  2. **History shards** (N D1 databases, e.g. 16, chosen by a hash of the company number): claims,\n     observations, fact versions. Point-in-time queries are always per company or per company list,\n     so each can be routed to its shard. The `Storage` interface already hides this; only the\n     adapter changes.\n  3. **Archive to R2:** observations and superseded fact versions older than 18 months move to R2 as\n     NDJSON partitioned by month and run; backtests that reach further back read R2 (slower, rare).\n- **The 50 MB result cap matters sooner than the size cap:** a few queries read every claim for a\n  field across all companies (`listUsableFor(null, …)` in module runs). They need paging. That's a\n  small fix and I'd do it in Phase 1 as hygiene.\n\n### Q3. Which existing part plays each new role\n\n| Design-note name | v2 name | What already plays the role (or \"new\") |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sensors | Scouts (P7) | Closest: website ingestion (`verification/src/sources/website`, untrusted-content rules), CH stream consumer, opportunity **signals** (`opportunity/src/signals.ts`). Scouts are new: they read external sources and emit `signals` rows only. |\n| Diagnoser, Predictor, Architect | Planner (P7) | Opportunity engine: `runModule`, framework notes (`brain/frameworks/*.md`), traced scores, `explain.ts`, `opportunities` table. The planner is new; the engine supplies scores. |\n| Activator | Outreach playbooks + post-reply playbook (P4) | `sme_outreach` (L1), `sme_letter` (L0), `processInbox`, follow-ups, contact cooldown. Post-reply playbook is new. |\n| Operators | Fulfilment Workflow (P4) | Pattern: `human_report`, `monitoring_subscription`, `watch_alert` (paid → L3 playbook → delivered). Site fulfilment is new. |\n| Governor | Safety layer | `ToolRegistry` (`infra/tools.ts`), `controls.ts` (caps, kill switches, queue pause), `autonomy.ts`, `contact.ts`, `outreach.ts` guardrails, licence lanes (`verification/query.ts`), copy rules and truth checker (`sme_outreach.ts`). |\n| Librarian, lessons store | Write-back + digests (P9) | `approvals`/`examples` (your edits), weekly digests (`opportunity/src/digest.ts`), weight suggestions (`calibrate.ts`), `docs/decisions.md`. Task write-back table is new. |\n| Toolsmith | — | Not in this build. |\n| Outcome ledger | `outcomes` (P1) | Existing `outcomes` table, extended. |\n| Budget envelope | Budget Durable Object (P3) | Wraps existing `checkSpend` + `caps` config; becomes the single source for limits. |\n| Review queue / DLQ items | Review queue | `/api/review` + Review page tabs; DLQ items become a fifth tab. |\n| Health page | Health | Extends Controls (no new page unless it gets crowded). |\n| Golden set (playbooks) | P5 | Built on `approvals`/`examples`; harness shared with the verification golden set. |\n\n## 4. Milestone A: schema, migrations, files\n\nMigrations continue from 0009. Each is plain SQL, applied to SQLite and D1, with the storage test suite\nrun on both (as today).\n\n**Phase 1 — Outcome ledger**\n- `0010_outcome_ledger.sql`: rebuild `outcomes` with: `task_id, playbook, playbook_version,\n  entity_id, opportunity_id, experiment_id, hypothesis_id, event` (CHECK on the funnel list:\n  `sent, delivered, qr_scan, demo_visit, reply, reply_positive, booking, checkout_started, paid,\n  refunded, churned`, plus the existing backtest events), `value_gbp, value_kind ∈ {actual, estimate},\n  assumption, ms_on_page, source` (adds `stripe, demo, inbox, simulator`), `observed_at, recorded_at,\n  synthetic`. Copy existing rows across (their events are kept; `synthetic` = 0).\n- `synthetic` column added to `tasks`, `outbox`, `letters`, `demo_visits`, `payments_ledger` so a\n  synthetic row can never feed learning even if it lands in the live database.\n- `0011_holdout.sql`: `outreach_cohorts (id, created_at, seed, holdout_share, size)`,\n  `cohort_members (cohort_id, entity_id, arm ∈ {ranked, holdout}, p_paid, ev_gbp, channel, reason)`.\n- `0012_demo_visits.sql`: `demo_visits (slug, visited_at, source ∈ {qr, link}, ms_on_page)` — no IP,\n  no user agent, no cookies.\n- `0013_review_minutes.sql`: `review_sessions (item_type, item_id, opened_at, decided_at, active_ms)`.\n- Config (`infra.json` / `products.json`, Zod-validated): `priors { reply_rate, close_rate,\n  first_year_value_gbp }` (labelled assumptions), `holdout_share` (0.10–0.20), `letter_ev_k`,\n  `skip_registered_office_flags`.\n- New code: `infra/src/ledger.ts` (record, funnel, lift + interval vs holdout, unit economics),\n  `infra/src/ev.ts` (P(paid) with Beta prior, point-in-time), `infra/src/cohort.ts` (deterministic\n  seeded holdout draw), demo-page beacon (tiny inline script posting time on page; works without it),\n  Review page timer.\n- Tests: synthetic rows excluded from every learning function; review decisions never read by scoring\n  (a test that fails if `ev.ts` or the scorer imports or queries `approvals`/`examples`/review tables);\n  point-in-time (outcomes recorded after T are invisible at T); holdout seed reproducible; holdout sends\n  pass the same compliance gates.\n\n**Phase 2 — Runtime**\n- `wrangler.jsonc`: Cron Trigger; Queues (`work`, `change-events`) each with a DLQ; Workflows\n  (`FulfilmentWorkflow` now; `ExperimentWorkflow`, `LoopWorkflow` stubs for Milestone B).\n- `0014_runtime.sql`: `dead_letters` (mirrors DLQ messages for the review queue), `trigger_stats`\n  (day, events_seen, rules_fired, model_calls).\n- Config: `trigger_rules` (field changes that matter: status → liquidation, new director, overdue\n  accounts…).\n- New code: `apps/api/src/queue.ts` (consumer), `apps/api/src/workflows/*.ts`, `infra/src/triggers.ts`.\n  The tick logic moves from `apps/agent-runner` into shared functions the Worker calls; the Node runner\n  stays for local use.\n- Kill switch: every Workflow step starts with a kill-switch check and ends the instance cleanly if on.\n\n**Phase 3 — Budgets**\n- `BudgetObject` Durable Object (SQLite-backed, one per playbook / experiment / global):\n  `reserve(amount, kind) → token | refused`, `settle(token, actual)`, `release(token)`, daily rollover.\n  `checkSpend` calls it; `run_log` stays the audit trail; a nightly reconcile compares the two.\n- `0015_spend_tiers.sql`: `owner_approvals (id, kind ∈ {s_tier, l3, budget_step, card}, subject,\n  from, to, approved_by, approved_at)`; config `spend_tiers { S0..S3: £ ceilings }`, `explore_share`.\n- An S-tier change can only be applied by an API route that writes `owner_approvals` from an\n  Access-authenticated request; no agent tool can call it (tool registry has no such tool; a test\n  asserts it).\n\n**Phase 4 — Taking money**\n- `0016_sites.sql`: `sites (id, entity_id, customer_id, status, subdomain, content, custom_domain,\n  published_at)`, `subscriptions (stripe_subscription_id, site_id, status, current_period_end)`,\n  `reply_triage (message_id, class, drafted_answer, task_id)`.\n- Stripe: Checkout in **subscription** mode with the setup fee as a one-off line item; webhook adds\n  `invoice.paid`, `customer.subscription.deleted`, `charge.refunded` (each idempotent, each written to\n  the ledger with the task that earned it).\n- Hosting: customer sites served by the same Worker by hostname (`<slug>.<sites domain>`), content in\n  D1 (small) and assets in R2. Custom domains via Cloudflare custom hostnames — **proposed for your\n  approval per site, never automatic** (and it's a cost line I'll check against current pricing at\n  build time).\n- `playbooks/site_fulfilment` (own autonomy track, starts L0), `playbooks/post_reply` (L0).\n- Human-minutes target and over-target card.\n\n**Gate reports:** `brain/build-reports/v2-phase-1.md` … `v2-phase-4.md`, then\n`v2-milestone-a.md` with config values to set, legal questions and the updated cutover checklist.\n\n## 5. Risks\n\n1. **Freeze conflict** (above). Building v2 in dry-run doesn't touch live data, but it is the feature\n   work you froze.\n2. **Holdout bias through review.** Holdout drafts go through the same approval gate. If you can see\n   which drafts are holdout, your approvals could bias the comparison. Proposal: holdout drafts look\n   identical in Review; lift is measured on everyone *assigned* to each arm (intent to treat); holdout\n   drafts you reject are reported as attrition. **Decision D4.**\n3. **Two runtimes during the move.** Moving the tick to Workers while keeping Node for local dev risks\n   drift. Mitigation: one set of pure functions, two thin callers, and the existing bundle test\n   extended to run a Workflow and a DO in Miniflare.\n4. **Budget counters in two places** (DO for limits, `run_log` for audit). A crash between reserve and\n   settle can strand budget. Mitigation: reservations expire; nightly reconcile; tests for both.\n5. **Point-in-time and synthetic leakage.** Every learning query takes an `asOf` and filters\n   `synthetic = 0`; enforced by shared query helpers plus tests that fail if a learning function reads\n   a table without them.\n6. **Legal (I'll flag, not decide):** visit tokens tie page views to a company → pseudonymous personal\n   data where the company is small or a sole trader (privacy notice needed; possibly a legal view);\n   holdout sends are still marketing and need the same PECR/legitimate-interest basis; subscription\n   terms and consumer cancellation rights (sole traders, partnerships); hosting liability for content\n   customers edit; introductions in regulated fields (Milestone B).\n7. **Hosting customer sites** is a new product surface (uptime, content, abuse). Kept minimal: static\n   one-page sites only, no customer scripts, publish needs your approval.\n8. **Workflows billing** now includes per-step charges (since Aug 2026). Small at our volume; I'll\n   show it on the health page.\n9. **Size of the build.** Milestone A is roughly the size of Phases 2 + 5 of v1 together. Milestone B\n   is larger and depends on real outcomes to mean anything (you said as much).\n\n## 6. Deferred (not built)\n\nDirector and company graph; agents that write new tools; connecting customers' own systems (accounts,\ninbox, CRM); licensed data sources; phone outreach; UK-scale storage split (design above only); anything\non frozen revenue paths.\n\n## 7. Decisions I need from you\n\n- **D1 — Freeze.** Does v2 override the freeze? If yes: build Milestone A now in dry-run, or after\n  cutover Stage 5?\n- **D2 — Revenue paths 3–6.** There's no status doc. `CLAUDE.md` has: 1 SME lead gen, 2 x402,\n  3 monitoring + human check (**built**), 4 Agent House / M&A (not built). Which paths are frozen?\n  My assumption if you don't say: freeze no new playbooks on 3 and 4; the built monitoring and human\n  check keep running as they are.\n- **D3 — Runtime.** Retire the planned Node container; run production on Cron + Queues + Workflows +\n  Durable Objects (Node kept for local dev and bulk ingestion). Update `CLAUDE.md` accordingly.\n- **D4 — Holdout in review.** Holdout drafts unlabelled in Review, intent-to-treat lift, rejections\n  reported as attrition.\n- **D5 — Report location.** v2 reports in `brain/build-reports/` (as asked), existing reports stay in\n  `docs/`. OK?\n- **D6 — Sites domain.** Which domain hosts claimed sites (e.g. `sites.ejtech.co.uk`)? A placeholder is\n  fine for the dry-run build.\n- **D7 — Taxonomy** (Milestone B only): the sixteen business functions in three tiers.\n\nValues you'll need to set before anything is live (placeholders in the build): `PRIOR_REPLY_RATE`,\n`PRIOR_CLOSE_RATE`, first-year value, letter `k`, holdout %, `EXPLORE_SHARE`, S-tier ceilings,\n`PRICE_SETUP_GBP`, `PRICE_MONTHLY_GBP`, human-minutes target, `MIN_EXPECTED_PAID` (Milestone B).\n"},{"path":"brain/build-reports/v3-milestone-c.md","body":"---\ntype: build-report\nmilestone: v3-C\ndate: 2026-11-17\nstatus: STOPPED — waiting for Archie\n---\n\n# v3 Milestone C: stop-point report\n\nMilestone C (v3 Phases 1–5) is built: the internal dashboard over the existing data. Every gate passes on\nsynthetic data, with fakes, with the kill switch on and Stripe in test mode. Nothing external happened.\n**I've stopped here, as planned.** Milestone D (Phases 6–10: agents and autonomy) waits for your go-ahead.\n\n## 1. Gate results\n\n| Phase | What it is | Gate | Report |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Shell (Home, Ideas, Board, Vault, CRM, Inbox, Controls), Home figures, CRM | **4/4 passed (synthetic)** | `brain/build-reports/v3-phase-1.md`, `docs/gates/v3-phase1.md` |\n| 2 | One Needs-you queue with safe defaults, the daily digest | **5/5 passed (synthetic)** | `v3-phase-2.md`, `docs/gates/v3-phase2.md` |\n| 3 | Inbox, the mailbox port (read and draft, no send), Email Routing handler, mailbox MCP endpoint | **3/3 passed (synthetic)** | `v3-phase-3.md`, `docs/gates/v3-phase3.md` |\n| 4 | Vault: search, backlinks, versioned edits, agent provenance, the ideas sheet (assumed) | **5/5 passed (synthetic)** | `v3-phase-4.md`, `docs/gates/v3-phase4.md` |\n| 5 | Controls → Keys: write-only keys, injection at the tool boundary, leak test, failing key pauses only dependants | **5/5 passed (synthetic)** | `v3-phase-5.md`, `docs/gates/v3-phase5.md` |\n\nRun any gate again with `pnpm gate:v3 --phase N`.\n\n**Tests:**\n- `pnpm check` is green: typecheck (including the Worker's), lint, and **392 tests in 53 files**.\n- Every gate also runs in CI.\n- The Worker builds with every binding (`wrangler deploy --dry-run`).\n- The phone layout test covers every page and tab at 390px and 1440px.\n\n**v3 rules, each enforced in code with a test:**\n\n| Rule | Where it's enforced | Test |\n|---|---|---|\n| 18 One decisions queue | every approve button goes through `decide()`; only `decisions.ts` creates owner approvals | gate 2 + static test |\n| 19 Safe defaults spend and send nothing; recorded as \"defaulted\" | `expireDecisions` applies the default with no approval | gates 2 and 5 |\n| 20 Keys are write-only | `keys.ts` is the only reader; values scrubbed; status-only table | gate 5 (leak test) + static tests |\n| 21 Agents write the Vault as new versions with provenance | `note_versions` append-only; `writeAgentNote` refuses without provenance | gate 4 |\n| 22 The holdout stays hidden | blind serializers on CRM, Inbox, the queue and the digest | gates 1–3 |\n| 23 Assumed numbers stay assumed | every tile labelled; ideas sheet labelled assumed and never read by scoring | gates 1 and 4 + static test |\n\n**Constraints from your brief, all kept:**\n- No landing page, sign-up or waitlist. The root URL is the dashboard. Public routes are unchanged, and\n  `/thanks` is still flagged.\n- No cookie banners, consent flows or new legal pages.\n- No new vendors or frameworks.\n- Every v1 and v2 guardrail test still passes.\n\n## 2. Unverified adapters (built to the docs or to a port, not yet run live)\n\n| Adapter | Why it's unverified | What verifies it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mailbox MCP endpoint** (`POST /api/mcp/mailbox`) | the MCP spec site was blocked from this build | connect one MCP client to it at cutover; until then it's only reachable from inside the Access-protected dashboard |\n| **Email Routing handler** (`email()` in the Worker) | built to the current Cloudflare docs; never received a real email | cutover Stage 1c: send a test email to the reply address |\n| **Secrets Store list call** (finds an existing secret's ID) | its response shape isn't shown in the docs; create and replace follow the docs exactly | cutover Stage 1b: the first save on the Keys page |\n| **Stripe subscription call** (from Milestone A) | Stripe docs blocked; the real call still **refuses to run**; event field names marked UNVERIFIED | allow `docs.stripe.com` for a session, or paste the four pages (see the Milestone A report) |\n| **Companies House stream** (from Milestone A) | docs blocked; polling does the work until `CH_STREAM_KEY` is set | cutover Stage 4 |\n| **Claude client** (from v1) | built to SDK 1.6.0's types; never called with a real key | cutover Stage 3 (`MODEL_LIVE` = \"1\" plus the key on the Keys page) |\n\n## 3. Every config value you need to set\n\nMilestone A's list still stands: priors, holdout share, letter EV, caps, spend tiers, sender details,\nclaim prices and terms, and triggers (`brain/build-reports/v2-milestone-a.md`, section 3). Milestone C adds:\n\n**`packages/config/src/infra.json` → `owner`**\n\n| Value | Now | What sets it |\n|---|---|---|\n| `hourly_gbp` | 40 | your real hourly value (decision D2). It drives \"your time\" costs on Home and stays labelled **assumed** until you change it |\n\n**`packages/config/src/decisions.json`** (how long each decision waits before its safe default)\n\n| Value | Now | |\n|---|---|---|\n| `deadline_hours.lead` / `print_batch` | 168 / 168 | a week |\n| `deadline_hours.draft` | 72 | three days, then the draft is cancelled (not sent) |\n| `deadline_hours.claim_publish` | 120 | five days, then \"not yet\" |\n| `deadline_hours.uncertain_effect` / `dead_letter` | 336 / 336 | two weeks |\n| `deadline_hours.data_conflict` / `quarantine` | 720 / 720 | a month |\n| `deadline_hours.spend_tier` / `autonomy` | 72 / 72 | |\n| `deadline_hours.key_failing` | 168 | the dependants stay paused regardless |\n| `max_open_leads` | 50 | how many lead decisions sit in the queue at once |\n| `digest_hour_utc` | 6 | when the daily digest is written |\n\n**`packages/config/src/keys.json`** lists six keys and the playbooks that depend on each:\n`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `CH_API_KEY`, `CH_STREAM_KEY`, `EMAIL_API_KEY`, `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` (test mode only),\n`STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET`. Check that the dependants match how you want pauses to work.\n\n**`apps/api/wrangler.jsonc` and Cloudflare**\n\n| Value | Kind | |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN`, `ACCESS_POLICY_AUD` | vars (already there, empty) | your Access app |\n| `CF_ACCOUNT_ID`, `SECRETS_STORE_ID` | vars (new) | for saving keys from the dashboard |\n| `CF_SECRETS_TOKEN` | Worker secret (the one you set by hand) | a token with **Account Secrets Store Edit only** |\n| `secrets_store_secrets` block | bindings | snippet in `docs/runbooks/keys.md` |\n| the reply address | Email Routing | e.g. `replies@<sending domain>` routed to the Worker (decision D1) |\n\n**Files only you can write:**\n- `brain/ideas/seed-500.csv`, your 500 scored ideas. The Vault shows it with every score labelled\n  assumed. The page tells you if it's missing.\n- `brain/status-and-vision.md` is a stub marked \"To write (Archie)\".\n\n## 4. Updated cutover checklist\n\n`docs/live-cutover.md` now has:\n\n- **Stage 1, v3 dashboard:**\n  - D1 migrations 0014–0017 (`/api/health` shows 17);\n  - the root URL opens the dashboard;\n  - Needs you and the digest;\n  - the Vault bundle, and adding the ideas sheet.\n- **Stage 1b, Keys (new):**\n  - create the Secrets Store and the narrow token;\n  - set the vars and bindings;\n  - make the first save on the Keys page, which verifies the list call;\n  - from then on, keys go in through the Keys page.\n\n  The runbook is `docs/runbooks/keys.md`.\n- **Stage 1c, Replies inbox (new):**\n  - the dedicated reply address, routed to the Worker by Email Routing;\n  - a test email;\n  - check the sending provider's terms allow B2B cold outreach before adding `EMAIL_API_KEY`.\n- **Stage 1d, GitHub settings (new, manual, decision D5):**\n  - **CODEOWNERS** on the safety-layer files (listed in the checklist);\n  - **branch protection** on `main`: pull request, code-owner review, CI, no force push, **no auto-merge**;\n  - the conditions if Claude Code ever runs from GitHub: only from your label or comment, never on a\n    schedule.\n\n## 5. Things to know\n\n- **Keys moved behind the key ring.** The Worker now reads the Claude, Companies House and Stripe keys\n  through it. Existing Worker secrets keep working as a fallback, so nothing breaks if you set keys the\n  old way first.\n- **A key a provider refuses** pauses only the playbooks that use it, and asks you in Needs you. Saving a\n  new key resumes them.\n- **Vault edits.** An edit made on the dashboard lives in D1 until it's committed to `brain/`. The page\n  labels it.\n- **Ideas and Board** are placeholders until Milestone D (Phase 6).\n\n## 6. What happens next\n\nNothing, until you say. Milestone D (v3 Phases 6–10: idea cards and the Board, agents proposing work,\nearned autonomy, brain pull requests, the loop) waits for your approval. Cutover Stages 0–5 can carry on\nin parallel. The new Stages 1b–1d are quick and unblock the inbox and keys.\n\nEvery commit is on `claude/nifty-curie-91l1nq`.\n"},{"path":"brain/build-reports/v3-phase-1.md","body":"---\ntype: build-report\nphase: v3-1\ndate: 2026-11-17\nstatus: gate passed (synthetic)\n---\n\n# v3 Phase 1 — shell, Home and CRM (gate report)\n\n## What changed\n\n**The navigation now has seven pages:** Home, Ideas, Board, Vault, CRM, Inbox, Controls. Old links redirect to wherever their\ncontent moved (`#/review` → Inbox, `#/letters` → Inbox → Letters, `#/facts` → CRM → Facts, `#/golden` →\nCRM → Verify, `#/metrics` and `#/overview` → Home, `#/company/<number>` → CRM company view). Ideas and\nBoard are placeholders until Milestone D; Vault arrives in Phase 4.\n\n**Home** (`/api/home`) shows the following, each read from the outcome ledger:\n- revenue;\n- monthly recurring revenue;\n- pipeline value;\n- sends and reply rate;\n- sales;\n- cost to acquire a customer and cost to serve;\n- margin;\n- ranked-versus-holdout lift with its 95% range;\n- your review minutes.\n\n**Every tile is labelled** as one of:\n\n| Label | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| real | comes from actual events |\n| estimated | comes from the conversion model |\n| assumed | rests on a placeholder, e.g. the £40/hour rate |\n| dry run | the sends were simulated |\n| SIMULATED | simulator rows are included |\n\nThere are filters for the date range, the playbook, and whether to include simulated rows. The old Overview\nand Metrics sit below the tiles as folded sections.\n\n**CRM** (`/api/crm`, `/api/crm/:id`, `/api/crm.csv`) has one row per company that we've touched or that has a\nlive lead. Each row shows:\n- **stage**: new, contacted, engaged, replied, claimed, customer, lost or opted out;\n- expected value (real for customers, estimated for leads);\n- last contact;\n- next step;\n- the agent that owns it.\n\nYou can search, filter by stage and export a CSV. The company view shows every contact (emails and letters),\nevery ledger event, the claimed site, the work done, opt-out status and the verified facts. The Facts browser\nand the verification golden set now live here as tabs (Facts, Verify).\n\nThere is **no CRM table**: the stage is always derived from the ledger, letters, claims, suppressions, leads\nand open tasks, so it can't drift from the records.\n\n**The holdout stays hidden** (rule 22). There is now one shared \"blind\" serializer (`packages/infra/src/blind.ts`). It's used by Review, the CRM and\nthe task view. It drops the arm and cohort from ledger rows and tasks.\n\n## Gate (`pnpm gate:v3 --phase 1`, docs/gates/v3-phase1.md)\n\n| Criterion | Status |\n|---|---|\n| All seven routes are in the navigation and render at phone and desktop width | passed (synthetic) |\n| Home figures reconcile to the ledger (revenue = paid − refunded, sends, sales) and every tile is labelled | passed (synthetic) |\n| The CRM shows a full history for a fixture company (letter → claim → paid → renewal → refund) | passed (synthetic) |\n| No company, task or message view reveals the holdout arm | passed (synthetic) |\n\nThe phone-layout test (`tests/mobile-layout.test.ts`) now covers every page and tab at 390 px and 1440 px, with\nand without data. It also fails if a navigation page is ever left out. `pnpm check` is green: 376 tests in\n47 files.\n\n## Config\n\n- `infra.json` → `owner.hourly_gbp` = **40**, marked as an assumption (decision D2). Set it, then set\n  `hourly_is_assumption` to false.\n\nNext: Phase 2, the Needs-you queue.\n"},{"path":"brain/build-reports/v3-phase-2.md","body":"---\ntype: build-report\nphase: v3-2\ndate: 2026-11-17\nstatus: gate passed (synthetic)\n---\n\n# v3 Phase 2: Needs-you queue and digest (gate report)\n\n## What changed\n\n**One queue for everything that needs you** (rule 18). It's at the top of Home, ranked by money at stake.\nEach item is a `decision`, with:\n- a summary;\n- the money at stake, labelled real or estimated;\n- the options, with what each one does;\n- the recommended option, highlighted;\n- the **safe default**;\n- a deadline.\n\nThe queue fills itself from 8 sources:\n\n| Type | From | Safe default |\n|---|---|---|\n| lead | proposed leads (top 50, skipping companies in the contact cooldown) | skip |\n| draft | email and reply drafts waiting for review | don't send (task cancelled) |\n| print_batch | pending print batches | don't print |\n| claim_publish | claimed sites ready to publish | don't publish |\n| uncertain_effect | external actions with an unknown outcome | leave blocked (never repeated) |\n| dead_letter | failed jobs | set aside |\n| data_conflict | fact conflicts (choose a value or leave) | leave |\n| quarantine | unmatched records | leave |\n\nRaising a spend tier and changing autonomy are decisions too. They're created and decided in one tap when\nyou ask for them.\n\n**One tap, one path.** Every choice goes through `decide()`\n(`packages/infra/src/decisions.ts`). It writes exactly one recorded approval, the same mechanism spend\ntiers and L3 already used, and then applies the option. The old approve buttons still work, but each one\nnow decides the matching queue item:\n- task draft, lead, print batch and claim publish;\n- uncertain action, dead letter and quarantine;\n- tier raise and promote.\n\nThe separate approval lists are gone:\n- **Review is removed.** Inbox → Drafts shows the queue filtered to drafts.\n- **Claimed sites and Runtime health** now link to the queue.\n- **Letters** keeps the batch details (PDF and flags) behind the batch's queue item.\n\n**Safe defaults** (rule 19). When a deadline passes, the item takes the option that spends nothing and sends\nnothing. It's recorded as \"defaulted\", with no owner approval and no approval-history row, so it can never\nlook like your decision. Home has a \"Recently defaulted\" list. Deadlines are in config\n(`packages/config/src/decisions.json`): leads 7 days, drafts 3, print batches 7, publish 5, data items 30.\n\n**Daily digest.** A short summary of yesterday is shown on Home: events, money earned and spent, decided,\ndefaulted and waiting. The cron builds it once a day after 06:00 UTC. Email delivery comes with the mailbox\nport and a real sender.\n\n**The cron** now syncs the queue and applies expired defaults every 5 minutes (job `decisions`).\n\n## Gate (`pnpm gate:v3 --phase 2`, docs/gates/v3-phase2.md)\n\n| Criterion | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Decisions from many sources appear in one list, ranked by money at stake (6 sources in the fixture) | passed (synthetic) |\n| An expired decision takes its safe default and is never counted as an approval | passed (synthetic) |\n| No approval can be created except through the decisions path (behavioural and static) | passed (synthetic) |\n| The queue keeps the holdout hidden | passed (synthetic) |\n| Daily digest: what happened, earned, spent and waiting | passed (synthetic) |\n\n`pnpm check` is green: 377 tests in 48 files. Migration 0014 adds `decisions` and `digests`. It also\nrebuilds `owner_approvals` to add `kind = 'decision'` and a `decision_id`, keeping the v2 rows.\n\n## Notes\n\n- **Expiry deliberately ends things.** For example, a draft that waits past 3 days is cancelled. If you'd\n  rather leave things waiting longer, raise the hours in `decisions.json`; no code change is needed.\n- **Fix-or-kill cards don't exist yet.** They were planned for v2 Phase 6 (frozen) and come in Milestone D.\n  The decision type is reserved for them.\n- **The playbook golden set** (Milestone D, captured from your decisions) will hang off this queue, as you\n  asked.\n\nNext: Phase 3, the Inbox and mailbox port.\n"},{"path":"brain/build-reports/v3-phase-3.md","body":"---\ntype: build-report\nphase: v3-3\ndate: 2026-11-17\nstatus: gate passed (synthetic); Email Routing handler and MCP transport unverified against live use\n---\n\n# v3 Phase 3: Inbox and mailbox (gate report)\n\n## What changed\n\n**The Inbox has five tabs:**\n\n| Tab | What it shows |\n|---|---|\n| Awaiting approval | email and reply drafts; the same items as the one queue, filtered to drafts |\n| Sent | outbox emails, including dry runs and blocked ones with their reason |\n| Replies | inbound mail, marked untrusted |\n| Agent drafts | drafts agents saved in the mailbox |\n| Letters | the print queue and claimed sites |\n\nEvery message links to its company in the CRM and shows its task. The company page shows its ledger\nevents. **Thread ids are never shown**, because they would reveal which arm an email came from (rule 22).\n\n**The mailbox** (decision D1) is one dedicated address on the sending domain, never your inbox. Messages are\nstored in `mail_messages` (migration 0015). They arrive two ways:\n\n- **Cloudflare Email Routing → the Worker's `email()` handler.** It reads the raw message with a small,\n  tested MIME reader (plain text, multipart, quoted-printable, base64, encoded subjects). It then links the\n  reply to the email it answers through the `replies+<outbox id>@` subaddress (or the In-Reply-To header),\n  and stores it as untrusted. The reply loop on Workers reads from here.\n- **Locally**, the fake inbox. Every reply the loop processes is also saved to the mailbox.\n\n**`MailboxPort`** has exactly three operations: list, read and create draft. **There is no send, delete or\nforward**, and a test fails if one is ever added.\n\n**Agents get the same three operations** (decision D6):\n- **As registry tools:** `mailbox_list`, `mailbox_read`, `mailbox_draft`. None is external. The operator\n  role can't read mail, so it never sees raw untrusted content. Reading returns content flagged untrusted.\n- **As an MCP endpoint:** `/api/mcp/mailbox` (behind Access). Tools `list`, `read`, `create_draft`; any\n  other tool name is an error.\n\nSending stays the `send_email` tool, behind the one queue.\n\n## Gate (`pnpm gate:v3 --phase 3`, docs/gates/v3-phase3.md)\n\n| Criterion | Status |\n|---|---|\n| A fixture mailbox shows the three tabs (awaiting approval, sent, replies), linked to company and task; no thread ids or arms exposed | passed (synthetic) |\n| An agent can draft but has no way to send (registry and MCP: `send`/`forward` → unknown tool; outbox unchanged; the operator can't read mail) | passed (synthetic) |\n| Prompt-injection replies (3 fixtures) trigger no tool call; nothing reaches the attacker's address | passed (synthetic) |\n\n`pnpm check` is green: 382 tests in 50 files. The Worker builds with the `email()` handler.\n\n**A bug the gate caught:** the MIME reader took the empty preamble before the first boundary as the text\npart. Fixed, with unit tests.\n\n## Unverified (listed for the Milestone C report)\n\n1. **MCP transport** (`/api/mcp/mailbox`). It's JSON-RPC with `initialize`, `tools/list` and `tools/call`,\n   written without access to the MCP specification, which is blocked from this environment. Check the\n   method names, the handshake and the transport before any external agent connects. Our own playbooks use\n   the registry tools, not MCP.\n2. **Email Routing on the real domain.** The handler follows the current Cloudflare docs, but it has never\n   received real mail. At cutover:\n   - add an Email Routing rule sending `replies@<sending domain>` (with subaddressing on) to the Worker;\n   - make the chosen sending provider use `replies+<outbox id>@…` as the Reply-To.\n3. **Sending provider:** not chosen (decision D1). When shortlisting, check its terms allow B2B cold outreach.\n\nNext: Phase 4, the Vault.\n"},{"path":"brain/build-reports/v3-phase-4.md","body":"---\ntype: build-report\nphase: v3-4\ndate: 2026-11-17\nstatus: gate passed (synthetic)\n---\n\n# v3 Phase 4: Vault (gate report)\n\n## What was built\n\n**The Vault page** (`/vault`) reads every note in `brain/`:\n- search by words in the note and by tag;\n- each note shows its front matter, the links out of it and the notes that link back to it\n  (`[[wikilinks]]` and ordinary relative Markdown links both count);\n- you can edit a note from the page.\n\n**Where notes come from.** Git holds the notes. On Cloudflare the Worker can't read the repo, so\n`pnpm cf:deploy` now bundles `brain/` into the Worker first (`pnpm gen:brain`). Locally the API reads the\nfolder directly.\n\n**Edits are versions, never overwrites (v3 rule 21).** Every edit, yours or an agent's, is a new row in\n`note_versions` (migration 0016). Nothing is ever updated in place, and the page shows the full history.\n- A note edited on the page is labelled **\"edited here, not yet in git\"** until it's committed.\n- An agent's note **must** carry provenance or it's refused:\n  - who wrote it, and when;\n  - which sources or ledger rows it rests on.\n- Labels carry through. A note built from a website shows **untrusted**.\n- Nothing writes agent notes yet. That's Milestone D; this phase builds and tests the rule it has to follow.\n\n**The ideas sheet.** `/vault/ideas` shows `brain/ideas/seed-500.csv` with your columns. Every score is\nlabelled **(assumed)** (v3 rule 23). A static test checks that no scoring, targeting or learning code\nreads the sheet.\n\n**The file isn't on the branch yet.** The page says so and lists the columns it expects. The gate uses a\nthree-row fixture with your exact columns. Add the real file to `brain/ideas/` and it appears after the\nnext deploy.\n\n## Gate (`docs/gates/v3-phase4.md`, run with `pnpm gate:v3 --phase 4`)\n\n| Criterion | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Search finds a note by its content (and by tag) | passed (synthetic) |\n| Links and backlinks resolve | passed (synthetic) |\n| An agent write creates a new version rather than replacing yours | passed (synthetic) |\n| Provenance on every agent-written note; a note without it is refused; labels carry through | passed (synthetic) |\n| The ideas sheet shows with every score labelled assumed | passed (synthetic) |\n\n**Other tests:**\n- The phone layout test now covers the Vault list, a note and the ideas sheet at 390px and 1440px.\n- New static test: the ideas sheet is never read by scoring code.\n\n## Things to know\n\n- **Committing an edit.** An edit made on the page lives in D1 until someone commits it to `brain/`.\n  The page shows the latest text either way.\n- **Writing `brain/status-and-vision.md`.** It is a stub marked \"To write (Archie)\", so the Vault has\n  something to link to.\n\nNext: Phase 5 (Controls and Keys).\n"},{"path":"brain/build-reports/v3-phase-5.md","body":"---\ntype: build-report\nphase: v3-5\ndate: 2026-11-17\nstatus: gate passed (synthetic); Secrets Store list call unverified against the live API\n---\n\n# v3 Phase 5: Controls and Keys (gate report)\n\n## What was built\n\n**Controls → Keys** (`/controls/keys`) lists every key the engine uses. For each one it shows:\n- what the key is for;\n- its status: not set, saved, working or failing;\n- when it was last used and when it last worked;\n- **which playbooks depend on it**.\n\nYou can paste a new value and save it. The page can never show a value back to you. The input clears\nstraight after saving, and the answer is just \"saved\".\n\n**Where values go (decision D4).** On Cloudflare:\n1. The page sends the value once to the Worker.\n2. The Worker passes it to **Cloudflare Secrets Store**, using a token that can only edit secrets\n   (`CF_SECRETS_TOKEN`).\n3. The Worker keeps nothing.\n\nLocally, keys come from `.dev.vars`, and anything saved on the page lasts for that session. The database\ntable `key_registry` holds **status only**: it has no column a value could go in.\n\n**How keys are used (rule 20):**\n- A tool declares the keys it needs (today only `send_email` needs one: `EMAIL_API_KEY`).\n- The **tool registry** fetches a key only for a *live* call. A dry run never touches a provider, so it\n  gets no key.\n- After the call, the registry **removes every injected value** from the tool's result, its error message\n  and the call log. Even a tool that echoes its key back can't pass it on, and tool results are the only\n  route from tools to a model.\n- The Claude client is built with its key at start-up. The key travels in the request header, never the body.\n- **Only `packages/infra/src/keys.ts` reads keys.** A static test enforces that. Another static test\n  stops the Worker reading any catalogue key straight from its environment.\n\n**When a provider refuses a key** (HTTP 401/403):\n- The key shows **failing**.\n- **Only the playbooks listed against it** (`packages/config/src/keys.json`) pause. Their tasks wait\n  untouched and don't take a queue slot; every other playbook keeps running.\n- One **\"Key failing\"** decision appears in Needs you. Its safe default is *keep them paused*.\n- Saving a new value resumes them and closes the decision. \"Try the same key again\" also resumes them,\n  for a provider outage.\n\nA missing key is refused **before anything happens**: no external action is recorded or attempted.\n\n**Moving keys across.** Existing Worker secrets keep working: the adapter reads a Secrets Store binding or\na Worker secret of the same name, so keys can move over one at a time. The Worker now reads the Stripe,\nCompanies House and Claude keys through the key ring, and rebuilds at most once a minute, so a newly\nsaved key takes effect without a deploy.\n\n## Gate (`docs/gates/v3-phase5.md`, run with `pnpm gate:v3 --phase 5`)\n\nThe gate uses a random canary value as the key, in a sandbox database with fake stores.\n\n| Criterion | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Keys are write-only: saved through the API, never returned; bad names and values refused | passed (synthetic) |\n| Injected only at the tool-registry boundary, only on live calls, scrubbed on the way out | passed (synthetic) |\n| **Leak test**: no key value in a model request, a log line, any database table or `brain/` | passed (synthetic) |\n| A refused key raises one decision and pauses only its dependants (real runner: an `sme_outreach` task waits while an `sme_letter` task runs to its approval gate) | passed (synthetic) |\n| A missing key is refused before anything happens | passed (synthetic) |\n\n**Other checks:**\n- I ran the gate five times in a row to make sure it's stable.\n- The Worker still builds with every binding (`wrangler deploy --dry-run`).\n- The phone layout test covers the Keys page.\n\n**A bug I caught on the way.** One gate check depended on luck. In about one run in three, the\n`sme_letter` task never started, so the \"only dependants pause\" check passed or failed by chance. I now\nrun each task through the real runner directly, on separate companies, and require the letter task to\nreach its approval step.\n\n## Unverified until cutover\n\n- **Secrets Store.** Saving a key for the first time uses a \"list secrets\" call to find whether the key\n  already exists. Its response shape wasn't in the docs, so it's marked unverified. Creating and replacing\n  a secret follow the docs exactly. Your first real save on the Keys page verifies it (cutover Stage 1b).\n- **Deploying the bindings.** Deploying with a Secrets Store binding to a secret that doesn't exist yet may\n  fail. Create each secret before adding its binding.\n\nNext: the Milestone C report, and then I stop.\n"},{"path":"brain/build-reports/v3-plan.md","body":"---\ntype: build-report\nmilestone: v3-plan\ndate: 2026-11-16\nstatus: waiting for Archie's approval (no code written)\n---\n\n# Agent Engine v3: plan (internal dashboard and judge-only autonomy)\n\n**No v3 code has been written.** This is step 0: the audit, the old → new page mapping, schema changes,\nrisks, effort, and my recommendation on each decision. When you approve, I build Milestone C (Phases 1–5)\nwithout stopping and then stop at the Milestone C report.\n\nStarting point: branch `claude/nifty-curie-91l1nq` at `b7f2ad5`. Milestone A is complete, `pnpm check` is\ngreen (374 tests), CI is green, and nothing is deployed.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Audit: what exists, per phase\n\n### Milestone C\n\n| Phase | Item | Status | What's there today |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 Shell | Navigation | partial | 7 tabs (Overview, Review, Letters, Facts, Metrics, Golden set, Controls) in a hash router (`App.tsx`). Needs regrouping into Home, Ideas, Board, Vault, CRM, Inbox, Controls. |\n| 1 Shell | Phone layout test | **exists** | `tests/mobile-layout.test.ts` checks every page at 390 px and 1440 px, seeded and empty, for zero sideways scroll. The page list needs updating. |\n| 1 Home | Analytics | partial | `/api/today` (drafts waiting, letters, conflicts, 7-day revenue/cost/margin), `/api/overview`, `/api/metrics`, `/api/lift`, `/api/economics` (cost, revenue, margin, minutes). Missing: MRR, pipeline value, reply rate, CAC, cost to serve, the real/estimated/synthetic label on every tile, date-range and playbook filters, one ledger-reconciled source. |\n| 1 CRM | Company list and stages | partial | Facts search (`/api/facts`), Company page (facts, claims, conflicts), `outcomeLedger`, `outbox`, `letters`, `suppressions`, `siteClaims`, `cohorts` all exist. Missing: a stage per company, expected value, last contact, next step, owner agent, filters and export. Stage can be **derived** from existing tables, so no new table is needed. |\n| 1 CRM | Holdout stays hidden | **exists** (pattern) | `blindTask` hides the arm in Review. Needs to become one shared serializer used by CRM and Inbox, with a test. |\n| 2 Queue | One decisions queue | partial | Approvals today come from **9 separate places**: task drafts, lead approvals, print batches, claim publish, spend tiers / L3 (`owner_approvals`), uncertain external effects, dead letters, fact conflicts, quarantine. There's no `decision` record, ranking, deadline or safe default. |\n| 2 Queue | Recorded-approval path | **exists** | `owner_approvals` (single use, Access identity recorded, created only by the dashboard API; a static test enforces this). It's extended to cover every decision. |\n| 2 Digest | Daily digest | partial | `scripts/digests.ts` (weekly, Node, writes `brain/digests/`). Missing: a daily digest built on Workers and shown on the dashboard. |\n| 3 Inbox | Outbound drafts / sent / replies | partial | `outbox` (sent, dry runs, blocked), drafts awaiting approval (tasks), the `Inbox` port (poll only) and `FakeInbox`, the `post_reply` playbook, letters and print batches. Missing: a `MailboxPort` (list, read, draft), stored replies, and the three tabs. |\n| 3 Inbox | Mailbox MCP server for agents | missing | No MCP code. The MCP spec site is **blocked** from this environment (checked). See decision 6. |\n| 4 Vault | Browser over `brain/` | missing | `brain/` has 2 frameworks, `taxonomy.md`, build reports, and empty `digests/` and `signals/`. No search, tags, backlinks or versions. **`brain/status-and-vision.md` is missing** (you said you'd add it; I'll create a stub and mark it \"to write\"). |\n| 5 Controls | Kill switches, budgets, runtime health | **exists** | Controls page (Budgets, Runtime health, Claims are there now). |\n| 5 Keys | Write-only keys, injected at the tool boundary, failure pauses dependants | missing | Keys are Worker secrets read directly in `worker.ts` / `runtime-worker.ts`. There's no registry, status page or leak test. |\n\n### Milestone D (summary; planned in detail at the Milestone C stop)\n\n| Phase | Status | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| 6 Ideas, Board, research agents | partial | The opportunity engine (`runModule`, framework notes, traced scores), `taxonomy.md`, and the trigger rules plus change feed (the \"scout\" input) exist. Missing: idea cards, planner, threads, hypothesis backlog, Board. |\n| 7 Standing rules, sampling | partial | Autonomy levels with auto-demotion, `approvals` and `examples` (raw material for the golden set), recorded approvals. Missing: rule records, sampling, the margin-window fix, playbook golden cases and hidden cases. |\n| 8 Coordinator, operations agent, circuit breakers | partial | Workflows engine, dead letters, recorded effects, health page and kill switches exist. Missing: the coordinator Workflow, the ops agent, rate-based breakers. |\n| 9 Hands-off delivery | partial | Claim fulfilment workflow, `post_reply`, `checkSiteDraft`. Missing: pre-publish site checks (links, forms, mobile), support agent, print and domain ports. |\n| 10 Money, self-improvement, x402 deploys | partial | Run-log costs, `unitEconomics`, payments ledger, `weights suggest`, x402 Worker. Missing: full vendor cost ledger, credit tracking, reinvestment rule, CSV export, bot PRs and path allowlist, deploy tasks, the weekly/monthly loop. |\n\n### Landing or marketing pages\n\n**None found.** The dashboard's `index.html` is the app shell. The root URL already serves the dashboard\nbehind Cloudflare Access (static assets). The Worker has no `/` route and no marketing, sign-up or\nwaitlist page.\n\nThe public routes are exactly the ones outreach and payments depend on, and **they stay unchanged**:\n- `/demo/*` (sample pages) and `/stop/*` (opt-out);\n- `/claim/*`, `/public/claim/*` and `/s/*` (claim this site and published customer sites);\n- `/public/checkout` and `/webhooks/stripe` (payments);\n- `/x402/*` (paid company checks).\n\nOne loose end, flagged but **not changed** because it's on a frozen path: the one-off checkout for the\nhuman report and monitoring sends customers to `/thanks`, which has no route.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Page mapping: old → new\n\n| Old page or panel | New home | How |\n|---|---|---|\n| Overview (data counts, \"Today\" panel) | **Home** | Data counts → a small \"Data\" strip. \"Today\" → the Needs-you queue preview and the digest. |\n| Metrics (lift, unit economics, verification accuracy, calibration, backtests, drift, pauses) | **Home** | Money and lift go on the main tiles. Verification quality goes in a \"Quality\" section lower on Home. |\n| Facts (search, list) | **CRM** | The company list *is* the facts search, plus stage, value and contact columns. |\n| Company page | **CRM → company view** | Adds the contact history, ledger events, opt-out status and claims. |\n| Review → leads to approve | **Needs you** (decision `lead`) | |\n| Review → email drafts | **Inbox → awaiting approval** | A filtered view of the queue. The approval itself is a decision, so there's no separate list. |\n| Review → conflicts, quarantine | **Needs you** (decision `data_conflict` / `quarantine`) + CRM company view | |\n| Letters → print queue | **Inbox**, letters filter | Approving a batch is a decision. |\n| Controls → Claims | **CRM** (stages claimed / customer) | Publishing is a decision. |\n| Golden set (hand-verify companies) | **CRM → \"Verify\" tab** | Each due batch appears in Needs you as one decision (\"verify 10 companies, about 15 min\"). |\n| Controls → kill switches, budgets, runtime health | **Controls** (unchanged), plus **Keys** | Uncertain actions and dead letters move to Needs you. |\n| — | **Ideas**, **Board** | Placeholders in Milestone C; built in Phase 6. |\n| — | **Vault** | Phase 4. |\n\nOld hash links (`#/review`, `#/letters`, `#/facts`, `#/metrics`, `#/golden`) will redirect to their new\nplace, so nothing you've bookmarked breaks.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Schema changes (Milestone C)\n\nThe existing names win; nothing is renamed. All migrations are plain SQL, tested on SQLite and D1.\n\n| Migration | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| `0014_v3_decisions.sql` | **`decisions`**: id, type, source_ref, summary, money_at_stake_gbp, options (json), recommended, safe_default, deadline, status (`open` / `decided` / `defaulted` / `superseded`), chosen_option, approval_id, synthetic, created_at, decided_at. Plus a unique open decision per (type, source_ref), so the same thing can't be queued twice. **`owner_approvals` is rebuilt** (SQLite can't change a CHECK constraint) to add `kind = 'decision'` and a `decision_id`. Every decision you make writes one approval through the existing single path. **`digests`**: day, body (json), created_at. |\n| `0015_v3_vault.sql` | **`note_versions`**: id, path, version, title, body, tags (json), author (`archie` / agent name / `git`), provenance (json: sources, ledger rows, flags `licensed` and `untrusted`), created_at. Append-only, so the newest version wins and nothing is overwritten. |\n| `0016_v3_mailbox_keys.sql` | **`mail_messages`** (the mailbox store behind the fake and the future inbound route): id, folder (`inbox` / `drafts` / `sent`), thread_id, from, to, subject, body, entity_id, task_id, untrusted, received_at. **`key_registry`**: name, status (`unset` / `ok` / `failing`), last_used_at, last_ok_at, last_error, used_by (json), updated_at. **It never stores a value.** |\n\nThe CRM needs **no table**: its stage, expected value, last contact and next step are derived in code\nfrom `outcomeLedger`, `outbox`, `letters`, `suppressions`, `siteClaims`, `opportunities` and `tasks`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. New files (Milestone C)\n\n**API**\n- `apps/api/src/routes/home.ts` (one ledger-reconciled analytics endpoint)\n- `crm.ts`, `decisions.ts`, `inbox.ts`, `vault.ts`, `keys.ts`\n\n**Domain code** (`packages/infra/src/`)\n- `decisions.ts`: create, rank, decide, expire to the safe default, and adapters from the 9 current sources\n- `crm.ts`: stage derivation\n- `analytics.ts`: Home figures with their real / estimated / synthetic labels\n- `digest.ts`\n- `mailbox.ts`: `MailboxPort` tools (list, read, draft); no send\n- `keys.ts`: registry, injection at the tool boundary, failure → pause dependants\n\n**Ports and config**\n- `packages/ports/src/mailbox.ts`: `MailboxPort` plus `FakeMailbox`\n- `packages/ports/src/keys.ts`: `KeyStore` port plus fake, and a Secrets Store adapter\n- `packages/config/src/keys.json`: the catalogue of key names and what uses each\n\n**Dashboard** (`apps/dashboard/src/`)\n- New pages: `Home.tsx`, `Crm.tsx`, `Inbox.tsx`, `Vault.tsx`, `NeedsYou.tsx`, `Keys.tsx`, plus placeholder `Ideas.tsx` and `Board.tsx`\n- Existing pages become sections of these, not rewrites.\n\n**Brain and scripts**\n- `brain/status-and-vision.md` (stub)\n- `scripts/gen-brain.ts`: bundles `brain/` for the Worker, the same way playbooks are bundled now\n\n**Gates**\n- `scripts/gate-v3-phaseN.ts` and `apps/api/src/gates/v3-phaseN.ts`, each with a CI test.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Decisions for the plan: my recommendations\n\n### D1. Mail provider: **build against `MailboxPort` with a fake (your default), and receive replies with Cloudflare Email Routing**\n\n- **Receiving.** Cloudflare Email Routing can deliver mail for a dedicated address on the sending domain\n  (for example `replies@mail.ejtech.co.uk`) straight into the Worker, which stores it in `mail_messages`.\n  That's **no new vendor**, it's on Cloudflare's free plan, and its docs are reachable. It is never your\n  personal inbox.\n- **Sending.** Keep this behind the existing `EmailSender` port and choose the provider later. Cloudflare's\n  own Email Sending is in public beta and its docs describe it as for **transactional** email. Cold\n  outreach isn't transactional, so check their terms before using it for outreach. A dedicated sending\n  provider is the safer default.\n- In Milestone C, I build the port, the fake and the inbound route. The adapter is marked unverified\n  until your domain is onboarded.\n\n### D2. Value of your time: **`OWNER_HOURLY_GBP` in config (your default), placeholder £40, labelled an assumption**\n\nIt's used by Home's \"cost to serve\", unit economics (your minutes × rate) and, later, idea-card ranking.\nThe dashboard shows it as an assumption until you set it.\n\n### D3. What \"pass\" on an idea does: **your default, plus two safety conditions**\n\nPass queues the idea. A test starts automatically **only if all of these hold**:\n- its budget is ≤ `AUTO_START_TEST_GBP`;\n- it fits inside this week's remaining explore share;\n- it stays within the playbook's spend tier;\n- the card has kill criteria and a sample size.\n\nOtherwise it raises a budget decision. (This is Phase 6, but the decision types are built in Phase 2.)\n\n### D4. How API keys are stored: **Cloudflare Secrets Store, written through a narrowly scoped token**\n\nFrom current Cloudflare docs:\n- A Worker's binding to a Secrets Store secret is **read-only** (`get()`), and a value can't be viewed\n  again once saved.\n- Writing needs an API token with the **\"Secrets Store Edit\"** permission only.\n- The alternative, writing Worker secrets through the Workers API, needs a token with Workers script\n  edit rights. **That token could also deploy code**, which is far broader.\n\n| | Secrets Store + scoped token | Encrypt with a Worker-secret key, store ciphertext |\n|---|---|---|\n| Value ever in D1 | **No** | Ciphertext would be in D1 (your requirement says values never reach D1) |\n| What the Worker holds to write | a token that can only edit secrets | the master key; if D1 and the key both leak, everything leaks |\n| Read path | binding `get()` at the tool boundary | decrypt at the tool boundary |\n| Rotation / audit | Cloudflare dashboard, audit log | our own code |\n| Catch | each key name needs a binding in `wrangler.jsonc` (a fixed catalogue in `keys.json`); Secrets Store was **beta** when announced, so confirm its status at cutover | more code of our own to get right |\n\n**Recommendation: Secrets Store.**\n- The dashboard's Keys page sends the value once to the Worker, which forwards it to Cloudflare's API\n  with the scoped token and keeps nothing.\n- The token itself (`CF_SECRETS_TOKEN`) is the one secret you set by hand with Wrangler.\n- Locally there's a fake store, and tests prove no value reaches a model request, log line, D1 or\n  `brain/`.\n- Agents never call `get()`: keys are injected inside the tool registry when a tool runs.\n\n### D5. Code changes without you writing prompts: **your default now (a \"build request\" card with a ready-to-run prompt); the GitHub upgrade only in Milestone D, with guard rails**\n\nCurrent docs (code.claude.com, GitHub Actions page) say the Claude Code GitHub Action can run with a\nprompt on any GitHub event, **including a schedule**. It has only the tools you grant it. It refuses\nruns triggered by a bot unless that bot is explicitly allowed.\n\nThe shared Claude GitHub App holds broad permissions, **including Workflows (read and write)**, which\nmeans it could edit CI files.\n\n**Is the upgrade safe? Yes, but only with all of these:**\n1. Trigger it from **your** action: a label you apply to the build-request issue, or an `@claude`\n   comment. The engine can open the issue, but its bot can't trigger the run. **No scheduled code\n   changes.**\n2. Give the workflow only `contents: write` and `pull-requests: write`, set `--max-turns`, a timeout,\n   and a separate API key with its own spend limit.\n3. Use **CODEOWNERS plus branch protection** so any change to the safety layer, `.github/` or CODEOWNERS\n   needs your review, and add a **CI path-guard** that fails a bot PR touching those paths.\n4. **Never auto-merge code PRs.** Phase 10's auto-merge covers `brain/**` only, and later the narrow\n   `playbooks/**` case you described.\n\nThe branch-protection and CODEOWNERS steps are GitHub settings I can't apply myself; they go on the\nMilestone D list for you.\n\n### D6 (mine). Mailbox MCP server: **build the three mailbox tools in the tool registry now; ship the MCP transport as an unverified adapter**\n\nThe MCP specification site is **blocked** from this environment, and our own playbooks call the Claude\nAPI through the tool registry, not over MCP. So:\n- The same three tools go into the registry: list, read and create draft. **There is no send, delete or\n  forward tool**, and a test proves it.\n- The MCP endpoint (`/mcp/mailbox`, behind Access with a service token) wraps those same tools. It's\n  marked unverified until someone can check the spec.\n- No new framework: Cloudflare's Agents SDK has MCP helpers, but it would be a new dependency.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Risks\n\n| Risk | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| **Holdout leaks through the new CRM or Inbox** | One shared \"blind\" serializer for every company and task view; a test scans every new API response for arm or cohort fields. |\n| **A second approval path survives the merge into one queue** | Old endpoints (`/api/tasks/:id/decision`, `/api/opportunities/:id/decision`, letters batch, claims publish, owner-approvals, effects resolve, dead letters) become thin wrappers that create and decide a `decision`. A static test checks that only `decisions.ts` writes `owner_approvals`, and a gate checks no approval exists without a decision. |\n| **Safe defaults change behaviour** | The safe default is always \"spends nothing, sends nothing\": for a draft, don't send; for a print batch, don't print; for publish, don't publish. A defaulted holdout draft counts in lift like a rejected one (intent to treat, as now). Deadlines are in config (`decisions.json`) so you can lengthen them. |\n| **Vault on Workers can't write to git** | Read `brain/` from a build-time bundle. Your edits and agent writes are stored as versions in D1, and Milestone D's brain-PR job turns them into pull requests. Until then, the Vault shows \"edited here, not yet in git\". |\n| **Secrets Store status or limits** | Behind a `KeyStore` port with a fake; the adapter is checked against docs before deploy; Worker secrets remain a fallback for the cutover. |\n| **Home figures disagree with each other** | Every tile computes from one ledger query module; the gate reconciles tiles to raw ledger rows on a fixture. |\n| **Scope creep in Milestone C** | Ideas and Board are placeholders only; no agents are built until Milestone D. |\n| **The phone-layout test gets slow with 7 pages × 2 widths × 2 datasets** | Already about 30 s; accept it and run the browser once per test file. |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Effort estimate (build sessions like the v2 ones)\n\n| Phase | Kind | Estimate |\n|---|---|---|\n| C1 Shell, Home, CRM | mostly regrouping and new queries | 1.5 |\n| C2 Needs-you queue and digest | new core (touches 9 approval sources) | 1.5 |\n| C3 Inbox, MailboxPort, mailbox tools/MCP | extension plus a new port | 1 |\n| C4 Vault | new, but simple | 1 |\n| C5 Keys | new, security-sensitive | 1 |\n| **Milestone C** | | **about 6** |\n| D6 Ideas, Board, research agents | largest new build | 2.5 |\n| D7 Standing rules, sampling, golden cases | | 1.5 |\n| D8 Coordinator, operations agent, breakers | | 1.5 |\n| D9 Hands-off delivery | | 1.5 |\n| D10 Money, auto-merge, x402 deploys, the loop | | 2.5 |\n| **Milestone D** | | **about 9.5** |\n\n---\n\n## 8. What I'll change in `CLAUDE.md` after approval\n\n- The v3 milestones: C to be built, then stop; D frozen until you approve after C.\n- New rules:\n  - one decisions queue, and no page may hold its own approval list;\n  - a safe default is never recorded as your approval;\n  - keys are write-only and injected at the tool boundary;\n  - agent writes to the Vault are new versions.\n- The commands for the v3 gates.\n\n## Decisions I need from you\n\n1. Approve the plan as written, or change any of D1–D6.\n2. Confirm the page mapping, especially Golden set → CRM \"Verify\" tab, and Metrics' quality sections →\n   Home.\n3. Optional: your hourly rate for `OWNER_HOURLY_GBP`. Otherwise it's £40, labelled an assumption.\n"},{"path":"brain/frameworks/lead_gen_sme_web.md","body":"---\nmodule: lead_gen_sme_web\nversion: 1\nuniverse:\n  members_of: lead_gen_sme_web_v1\n  region: Greater Manchester\n  headcount: [5, 100]\n  status: active\nlanes: [open]\nconfidence_floor: 0.7\nsignals:\n  website_age_years: { weight: 0.30 }\n  hiring_momentum: { weight: 0.25 }\n  filing_health: { weight: 0.15, invert: true }\n  director_change_90d: { weight: 0.30 }\nthreshold: 0.6\noutcome: { event: positive_reply, window_days: 30, value_field: deal_value_gbp }\nexplore_share: 0.10\neconomics: { deal_value_gbp: 1500, base_win_rate: 0.02 }\n---\n\n# Lead gen: SME websites (Greater Manchester)\n\nArchie's framework for finding small businesses likely to buy a new website.\n\n- **Old or missing website** (30%): the older the site looks, the stronger the need. No website at all scores as the oldest.\n- **Hiring** (25%): businesses advertising jobs are growing and spending.\n- **Filing health, inverted** (15%): companies behind on filings may need help getting organised.\n- **New director in the last 90 days** (30%): new people bring new budgets and fresh eyes.\n\nUnknown signals score neutral (0.5): missing data is never a penalty.\n\n`economics` sets expected value: P(win) = 2% × score ÷ 0.5 (capped at 100%), × £1,500 average deal.\nBoth are placeholders until Phase 3 measures real outcomes.\n\nWeights change only when Archie accepts a suggested change (written below by the system).\n"},{"path":"brain/frameworks/x402_discovery.md","body":"---\nmodule: x402_discovery\nversion: 1\nuniverse:\n  members_of: lead_gen_sme_web_v1\n  region: Greater Manchester\nlanes: [open]\nconfidence_floor: 0.5\nsignals:\n  x402_interest: { weight: 0.5 }\n  director_change_90d: { weight: 0.25 }\n  ownership_change_180d: { weight: 0.25 }\nthreshold: 0.5\noutcome: { event: positive_reply, window_days: 30, value_field: deal_value_gbp }\nexplore_share: 0.10\neconomics: { deal_value_gbp: 1500, base_win_rate: 0.02 }\n---\n\n# x402 discovery\n\nAI agents pay to check a company when they're about to do business with it. A company being checked by\nseveral different agents is probably trading actively and changing — a good moment to offer help.\n\n- **x402 interest** (50%): distinct paying agents in the last 30 days (from our own request log).\n- **New director** (25%) and **ownership change** (25%): change brings new budgets.\n\nApproved leads go to the `sme_outreach` playbook (pilot until a backtest shows ≥ 3× lift).\n"},{"path":"brain/status-and-vision.md","body":"---\ntype: vision\nstatus: stub\nauthor: archie\n---\n\n# Status and vision\n\n**To write (Archie).** This note was created as a placeholder in v3 Phase 4 because the plan expects it in\nthe Vault. It's yours to fill in: where the business is now, where it's going, and the revenue paths in\norder (see `brain/build-reports/v3-plan.md` and [[taxonomy]]).\n"},{"path":"brain/taxonomy.md","body":"---\ntype: taxonomy\nversion: 1\nadded: 2026-11-16\nused_by: v2 Milestone B (Phase 7 signals and opportunity cards)\n---\n\n# Business-function taxonomy\n\nSixteen functions in three tiers. Every signal and opportunity card carries exactly one node, so\nresults can be compared by function. Supplied by Archie (decision D7, 2026-11-16).\n\n## Tier 1 — revenue-critical\n- sales\n- marketing\n- customer service and success\n- operations and delivery\n- supply chain and logistics\n- product and R&D\n\n## Tier 2 — control-critical\n- finance\n- legal, risk and compliance\n- IT and security\n- HR\n\n## Tier 3 — enabling\n- procurement\n- data and analytics\n- strategy and M&A\n- communications, IR and ESG\n- facilities and property\n- transformation office\n"}];
